1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,267
(upbeat music)

2
00:00:02,267 --> 00:00:04,967
♪ In the not too
distant future ♪

3
00:00:04,967 --> 00:00:08,167
♪ Somewhere in time and space ♪

4
00:00:08,167 --> 00:00:11,200
♪ Mike Nelson and
his robot pals ♪

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,200
♪ Are caught in
an endless chase ♪

6
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:15,867
♪ Pursued by a woman ♪

7
00:00:15,867 --> 00:00:17,400
♪ Whose name is Pearl ♪

8
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,700
♪ An evil gal who wants
to rule the world ♪

9
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:23,500
♪ She threw a few
things in her purse ♪

10
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:24,733
♪ And in her rocket ship ♪

11
00:00:24,733 --> 00:00:27,533
♪ She hunts him all
across the universe ♪

12
00:00:27,533 --> 00:00:30,033
- [Pearl] I'll get you!

13
00:00:30,033 --> 00:00:33,033
♪ I'll send him cheesy movies ♪

14
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:35,233
♪ The worst I can find ♪

15
00:00:35,233 --> 00:00:36,600
♪ La la la ♪

16
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:39,200
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

17
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,500
♪ And I'll monitor his mind ♪

18
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:43,633
♪ Now keep in mind ♪

19
00:00:43,633 --> 00:00:45,433
♪ Mike can't control ♪

20
00:00:45,433 --> 00:00:47,900
♪ When the movies begin or end ♪

21
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:48,900
♪ La la la ♪

22
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,600
♪ He'll try to keep his sanity ♪

23
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,567
♪ With the help of
his robot friends ♪

24
00:00:57,367 --> 00:00:58,400
♪ Robot roll call ♪

25
00:00:58,400 --> 00:00:59,533
♪ Cambot ♪
- You're on.

26
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:01,100
♪ Gypsy ♪

27
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:02,633
♪ Tom Servo ♪
- Check me out.

28
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:04,133
♪ Crow ♪

29
00:01:04,133 --> 00:01:07,300
♪ If you're wondering how
he eats and breathes ♪

30
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:09,600
♪ And other science facts ♪

31
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:10,567
♪ La la la ♪

32
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:11,600
♪ Just repeat to yourself ♪

33
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:13,767
♪ It's just a show ♪

34
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:16,667
♪ I should really just relax ♪

35
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:20,900
♪ For Mystery Science
Theatre 3000 ♪

36
00:01:24,233 --> 00:01:27,967
(doors creaking and closing)

37
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:38,667
- Hi everyone, welcome to
the Satellite of Love here.

38
00:01:38,667 --> 00:01:40,100
- Hi.

39
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,400
- Probably wondering
what's up with Crow here.

40
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:42,633
Well, I'll let him
explain, go ahead.

41
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:43,800
- Explain what?

42
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,900
Oh yeah, the nest.

43
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:48,000
Yeah, it turns out
that the top of my head

44
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,633
provides a perfect nesting
site for the rare Spix's macaw.

45
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:53,767
I've known that for some time,
but this is the first year

46
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:55,833
I've had an actual
brooding mother.

47
00:01:55,833 --> 00:01:57,900
- See, what I don't get
is how you cannot notice

48
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:00,100
them building a nest on
the top or your head.

49
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:02,367
- Well, I was aware of
some activity, Mike.

50
00:02:02,367 --> 00:02:04,300
But you know, it's
the top of my head.

51
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:06,867
I suppose you know every
detail at all times

52
00:02:06,867 --> 00:02:09,267
of everything happening
at the top of your head?

53
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:12,100
- I guess not.
- Yeah.

54
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:13,033
- When does that
one hatch anyway?

55
00:02:13,033 --> 00:02:14,500
- Well, pretty soon, Mike.

56
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:15,600
It's been three weeks and
to tell you the truth,

57
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:17,300
I'll be a little relieved.

58
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:20,167
The Spix's macaw is
not a small bird.

59
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:22,367
So when the mother's actually
here, sitting on my head

60
00:02:22,367 --> 00:02:25,600
with that brooding
spot, all pink and warm.

61
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,000
There's no feathers,
it just, whoa, whoa.

62
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,067
Here she comes again.

63
00:02:29,067 --> 00:02:30,667
(bird squawking)

64
00:02:30,667 --> 00:02:31,600
Damn, another one.

65
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:34,067
- Great. We'll be right back.

66
00:02:34,067 --> 00:02:35,933
- Mike, would you
mind doing this one?

67
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:38,667
I feel like I've done my
part for the Spix's macaw.

68
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:41,267
- Well, you're the one with
the perfect nesting site head.

69
00:02:41,267 --> 00:02:42,100
(Crow sighs)

70
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:44,667
(upbeat music)

71
00:02:48,367 --> 00:02:49,267
- [Crow] Ack.

72
00:02:49,267 --> 00:02:50,700
Get away, you rebel.

73
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:51,867
Cease and desist.

74
00:02:51,867 --> 00:02:53,033
Back, I say.

75
00:02:54,133 --> 00:02:55,867
Okay then, you mongrels.

76
00:02:55,867 --> 00:02:58,767
(gun shotsbanging)

77
00:03:01,767 --> 00:03:04,000
(laughing) Eat it.

78
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,600
- Crow, everything all right?

79
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,600
- Oh yeah, I had a little
weasel problem there, Mike.

80
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,467
They were trying to steal
my poor little eggs.

81
00:03:10,467 --> 00:03:13,100
I never knew weasels
could be such weasels.

82
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:15,833
- Pearl's goons are calling.

83
00:03:15,833 --> 00:03:17,000
Yellow?

84
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:18,400
- What, do you
want a little more?

85
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,067
- No.

86
00:03:20,067 --> 00:03:21,300
- All right, Sporto.

87
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:23,367
This time, you are
one dead monkey.

88
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:28,367
One, two, three. (grunts)

89
00:03:28,367 --> 00:03:30,000
Oh god.

90
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,500
All right, best out of 99, then.

91
00:03:33,933 --> 00:03:38,500
- Oh, hello, disturbingly
unhairy ones. (laughing)

92
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:40,333
The lovely Pearl is still gone.

93
00:03:40,333 --> 00:03:42,633
- Yay!
- Wee, woo hoo!

94
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:43,467
- Yes.

95
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:47,233
- Oh yeah, that's a shame.

96
00:03:47,233 --> 00:03:48,067
- Dog gonnit.

97
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:49,567
- Really.

98
00:03:49,567 --> 00:03:52,267
- But stay tuned for this
important message from her.

99
00:03:52,267 --> 00:03:54,100
Okay, rubber arms, back to work.

100
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:55,067
- Yes.

101
00:03:55,067 --> 00:03:56,333
(keypad beeping)

102
00:03:56,333 --> 00:03:58,800
Accessing satellite
relay to Los Angeles.

103
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,033
Come in, Pearl.

104
00:04:01,033 --> 00:04:02,200
Pearl, come in.

105
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:03,133
Come in, Pearl.

106
00:04:03,133 --> 00:04:03,933
Pearl, come in, can you read me?

107
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:05,867
- Calm down, brainiac.

108
00:04:05,867 --> 00:04:07,100
Yeah, hi, everyone.

109
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:09,533
I flew the van out here
to LA to try and find

110
00:04:09,533 --> 00:04:11,900
some more movies to
torture you guys with.

111
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:13,333
Thought I'd consult an expert.

112
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:15,667
So I took and went and
gone and got this guy here,

113
00:04:15,667 --> 00:04:17,300
from Entertainment Tonight.

114
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:18,133
- Hello, Mike.

115
00:04:18,133 --> 00:04:19,467
Hello, small robots.

116
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:20,600
How can I help?

117
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:21,800
- Leonard Maltin?

118
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:23,900
Pearl, you are connected, whew.

119
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:25,600
- Excuse me, Mister
Maltin, what do you make

120
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:27,600
of the stylistic
resurgence of Italian

121
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,200
neo-realist filmmaking in the
vein of De Sica's Umberto D?

122
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:32,467
- Yeah, Maltin.

123
00:04:32,467 --> 00:04:34,600
Is it true their working
on yet another remake

124
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:36,533
of "The Fish That
Saved Pittsburgh?"

125
00:04:36,533 --> 00:04:37,733
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

126
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:40,400
- Stop bugging Mr.
Siskel with questions.

127
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:41,233
- Maltin.

128
00:04:41,233 --> 00:04:42,567
- He's helping me.

129
00:04:42,567 --> 00:04:45,033
So, what do you
recommend, Roger?

130
00:04:45,033 --> 00:04:46,500
- Leonard.

131
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:49,667
I'd highly recommend the
1961 monster movie, "Gorgo."

132
00:04:49,667 --> 00:04:52,167
It's sort of a British
take on the Godzilla thing.

133
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,367
- Mr. Maltin, is
"Gorgo" really that bad?

134
00:04:56,367 --> 00:04:59,767
I mean, you're not really
gonna hurt us, are ya?

135
00:04:59,767 --> 00:05:01,267
- Well, that's a matter
of opinion, Mike.

136
00:05:01,267 --> 00:05:02,667
Now, I actually like Gorgo.

137
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:04,500
But when we reviewed
it for my number one

138
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,100
best selling movie
and video guide,

139
00:05:06,100 --> 00:05:09,233
it put two of my assistant
editors into intensive care.

140
00:05:09,233 --> 00:05:10,733
So who knows?

141
00:05:10,733 --> 00:05:12,600
Besides, Pearl promised me
a light lunch at Skybar.

142
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:14,033
- No.

143
00:05:14,033 --> 00:05:16,600
I said one appetizer
only, Ebert.

144
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:17,533
- Maltin.

145
00:05:17,533 --> 00:05:19,067
- Okay, Mr. Maltin Ebert.

146
00:05:19,067 --> 00:05:22,000
Don't push your luck or it's
a small decaf at Starbucks.

147
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:22,833
- Well.

148
00:05:22,833 --> 00:05:23,867
- Brainguy.

149
00:05:23,867 --> 00:05:25,733
- Oh, yes Pearl?

150
00:05:25,733 --> 00:05:26,567
Do you mind?

151
00:05:26,567 --> 00:05:28,600
- Please, not at all.

152
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:30,233
(eerie music)

153
00:05:30,233 --> 00:05:31,533
- Okay.

154
00:05:31,533 --> 00:05:33,267
And go.

155
00:05:33,267 --> 00:05:35,333
(grunts)

156
00:05:37,133 --> 00:05:38,633
- Hey Crow, your eggs are gone.

157
00:05:38,633 --> 00:05:41,667
- Oh yeah, they got taken away
by egg protective services.

158
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:43,500
I accidentally made an
omelet in front of them.

159
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:44,833
Kinda spooked 'em.

160
00:05:44,833 --> 00:05:45,667
- Yeah, well, I hope
you get to visit.

161
00:05:45,667 --> 00:05:46,500
(alarm blaring)

162
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:47,733
- Oh, we got movie time!

163
00:05:47,733 --> 00:05:50,033
- Curse you, Maltin!

164
00:05:50,033 --> 00:05:53,867
(doors creaking and thudding)

165
00:06:01,633 --> 00:06:03,667
- Wow.
- Right down here.

166
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:06,733
- (murmuring) London, England.

167
00:06:06,733 --> 00:06:09,000
Yeah, which is paid
for by exorbitant taxes

168
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,167
on Rod Stewart
and Pete Townsend.

169
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:17,200
- [Tom] Wait, I just
got three words into it.

170
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:18,967
- What a terrible
name for a movie.

171
00:06:22,667 --> 00:06:25,767
- The vice president's
unimaginative campaign slogan.

172
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:29,300
- Good masonry
work on the titles.

173
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:32,133
- Yeah it was done by the guys
at Mark Seven Productions.

174
00:06:33,967 --> 00:06:34,967
- [Tom] Aww.

175
00:06:38,033 --> 00:06:43,033
♪ Oh Gorgo boy, the
gore, the gore is going ♪

176
00:06:44,267 --> 00:06:47,100
♪ From gore to gore ♪

177
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:50,667
- Fa Young loves Fa Young.

178
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:58,667
- You know, name your
kid Muir Mathieson,

179
00:06:58,667 --> 00:07:01,633
he's pretty much destined to
conduct the Sinfonia of London.

180
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:03,633
- The lost stooge.

181
00:07:09,433 --> 00:07:11,200
- Yeah, yeah, and
all these other goons

182
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:13,067
from the sceptered isle.

183
00:07:17,867 --> 00:07:22,033
♪ Down with the
old folks at home ♪

184
00:07:24,267 --> 00:07:25,333
- Who's Paul Robison?

185
00:07:25,333 --> 00:07:26,933
Lovely.

186
00:07:26,933 --> 00:07:29,433
- Hawaii 5-0 use to have
really different credits.

187
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:36,500
- Frank King, Maurice King.
- Administration.

188
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:38,033
- [Mike] Did Frank
sneak in afterwards

189
00:07:38,033 --> 00:07:40,367
and put that little dig
under Maurice's name?

190
00:07:42,367 --> 00:07:44,733
- I think that ship's PC
valve is a little off there.

191
00:07:46,467 --> 00:07:47,800
- Without a hard tack!

192
00:07:52,167 --> 00:07:53,900
- Poop Deck Pappy,
the real story.

193
00:07:57,033 --> 00:07:59,633
- Sam, it's looking
terrible ugly.

194
00:07:59,633 --> 00:08:00,800
- [Tom] Well thanks a lot.

195
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:02,167
- The men are getting edgy.

196
00:08:02,167 --> 00:08:03,800
- Yeah, so am I.

197
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:06,233
- [Crow] Need any freelance
frog matting services?

198
00:08:06,233 --> 00:08:08,133
- Come on Joe, make
it snappy will ya?

199
00:08:10,967 --> 00:08:12,067
Come on, let's get outta here.

200
00:08:12,067 --> 00:08:13,433
- Now wait a minute Sam.

201
00:08:13,433 --> 00:08:14,267
There's thousands of
dollars worth of stuff

202
00:08:14,267 --> 00:08:16,333
in that old rust bucket.

203
00:08:16,333 --> 00:08:17,433
I'm not getting outta
here without it.

204
00:08:17,433 --> 00:08:18,700
- Are you kiddin'?

205
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:20,633
- [Tom] We have to go
find Robert Denbey.

206
00:08:20,633 --> 00:08:22,200
- [Man] Stop worrying.

207
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:24,033
I'll stake it out and be
back up in 10 minutes.

208
00:08:24,033 --> 00:08:25,500
- [Crow] I should moisturize.

209
00:08:30,633 --> 00:08:32,567
- [Mike] I think the
pool guy over-chlorinated

210
00:08:32,567 --> 00:08:33,700
the ocean here.

211
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:36,567
- [Tom] Oh, this new
cool ranch flavored

212
00:08:36,567 --> 00:08:37,900
scuba air isn't very good.

213
00:08:45,233 --> 00:08:48,000
- [Crow] The legendary lost
treasure of Captain Crunch.

214
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:54,067
- [Tom] Oh look,
he carries his own

215
00:08:54,067 --> 00:08:55,933
little expresso machine
on his back with him.

216
00:08:57,533 --> 00:08:59,233
- [Crow] Somebody's got
to get on the other side

217
00:08:59,233 --> 00:09:01,167
of that boat or we're
in trouble, guys.

218
00:09:04,433 --> 00:09:06,167
- [Man] He's been
down half an hour.

219
00:09:06,167 --> 00:09:07,600
- I know these waters well Sam.

220
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:09,233
I've never seen the
like of this before.

221
00:09:09,233 --> 00:09:12,133
- [Mike] Just puts
my hate out of shape.

222
00:09:12,133 --> 00:09:13,067
- Get me a lung.

223
00:09:13,067 --> 00:09:15,700
I'm going down to get him.

224
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:16,533
He's come up!

225
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:20,000
- [Tom] I put the propane
tanks on by mistake!

226
00:09:23,033 --> 00:09:24,700
(eerie music)

227
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:27,000
- [Crow] It's Robert
Maxwell, he's alive!

228
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:33,100
- [Man] Move it forward, Derek!

229
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:35,300
- [Mike] Our game of Risk!

230
00:09:39,533 --> 00:09:41,433
- [Crow] Mike, Servo,
for what it's worth,

231
00:09:41,433 --> 00:09:42,567
my heart will go on.

232
00:09:42,567 --> 00:09:43,400
- [Mike] Good.

233
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:44,233
- [Crow] Okay.

234
00:09:49,567 --> 00:09:50,767
- [Mike] You know, when
this boat was launched,

235
00:09:50,767 --> 00:09:53,267
it was promoted as
pretty damn sinkable.

236
00:09:56,600 --> 00:09:58,100
- [Tom] The HMS Over-Easy.

237
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:03,733
- [Crow] Yeah, it's rough.

238
00:10:03,733 --> 00:10:05,700
But is it really too
rough to feed us?

239
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:09,400
- [Mike] I'm
drowning and I'm wet.

240
00:10:14,733 --> 00:10:16,267
- [Tom] Gary, I'm steering!

241
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:20,900
- [Mike] Great, now our
toothpick sculpture.

242
00:10:23,167 --> 00:10:24,600
- [Tom] Let go!

243
00:10:28,433 --> 00:10:30,400
- [Mike] Man, this is
worse than when Australia

244
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:31,833
formed right next to us.

245
00:10:34,633 --> 00:10:36,333
- [Crow] Don't worry, sir,
turns out Shelly Winters

246
00:10:36,333 --> 00:10:37,500
is a very good swimmer.

247
00:10:40,933 --> 00:10:42,967
- [Mike] And?

248
00:10:42,967 --> 00:10:44,467
Oh, they're fine.

249
00:10:44,467 --> 00:10:46,100
♪ They got into port ♪

250
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:47,667
♪ And everyone was okay ♪

251
00:10:47,667 --> 00:10:51,867
♪ They went out for
lunch and felt better ♪

252
00:10:51,867 --> 00:10:52,900
- [Tom] Hey, Mike Nelson.

253
00:10:52,900 --> 00:10:54,133
- [Mike] Hey, Tom Servo.

254
00:10:54,133 --> 00:10:55,300
- [Tom] No, I mean...

255
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:56,100
Nevermind.

256
00:10:59,867 --> 00:11:00,700
Dummy.

257
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:01,767
- [Mike] Why?

258
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:07,167
Well, I'm sure it's fine.

259
00:11:07,167 --> 00:11:09,200
I don't know much about
propellers anyway.

260
00:11:10,467 --> 00:11:11,767
- [Tom] I guess I
shouldn't have thrown

261
00:11:11,767 --> 00:11:12,933
the captain's hat overboard.

262
00:11:12,933 --> 00:11:14,167
He wasn't pleased.

263
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:18,100
- [Crow] Well, there's
water in the basement.

264
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:20,233
The carpet's all ruined.

265
00:11:20,233 --> 00:11:22,100
- Not as bad as I thought, Joe.

266
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:23,700
- [Joe] How long to
get her into shape.

267
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:27,133
- Rutter stock's bent,
couple of sprung plates.

268
00:11:27,133 --> 00:11:28,367
- [Joe] Uh-huh.

269
00:11:28,367 --> 00:11:30,133
- At best on deck,
three or four days.

270
00:11:30,133 --> 00:11:31,367
- And we're out of fresh water.

271
00:11:31,367 --> 00:11:33,000
- [Mike] We'll have to
drink concentrate, then.

272
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,567
- Let's go ashore and see
what they've got, eh Sam?

273
00:11:38,167 --> 00:11:40,900
- [Tom] Yes, let's put on
some Old Spice and go ashore.

274
00:11:41,867 --> 00:11:45,200
(Tom whistling)

275
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:48,500
- [Crow] There's dead
cows in the water.

276
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:53,433
- Look there.

277
00:11:53,433 --> 00:11:55,900
- [Tom] A tuna
swallowed an armadillo.

278
00:11:55,900 --> 00:11:57,867
- I've never seen
anything like that before.

279
00:11:57,867 --> 00:12:00,733
- [Mike] Some kind of sealecanth
death cult or something,

280
00:12:00,733 --> 00:12:01,867
I don't know.

281
00:12:03,567 --> 00:12:06,400
- [Tom] Let's sell it to
the Japanese for $60.000.

282
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:10,800
- The whole ocean bottom
must of been torn up.

283
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:13,200
- [Crow] Ick, can I wipe
on your jacket, please?

284
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:18,000
- [Mike] Wow, new island
got settled very quickly.

285
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:19,000
- [Crow] Yeah.

286
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:20,000
- [Tom] Lice for sale!

287
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:21,367
Chiggers and lice!

288
00:12:27,333 --> 00:12:29,233
- Looks like they
were pretty hard hit.

289
00:12:29,233 --> 00:12:30,767
- Yeah.

290
00:12:30,767 --> 00:12:31,700
- [Mike] Poor dopes, they
appear every hundred years

291
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:33,067
and get hit by a huge storm.

292
00:12:33,067 --> 00:12:35,800
- Hi, is there a harbor
master we can talk to?

293
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:37,200
(speaking foreign language)

294
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:39,300
- [Tom] Something
about a kinnish?

295
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:40,467
- Gaelic, huh?

296
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:43,733
- Didn't sound like
welcome in any language.

297
00:12:43,733 --> 00:12:46,500
- [Crow] So how do you get
your shirt so white, Shamus?

298
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:52,533
(knocking on door)

299
00:12:52,533 --> 00:12:53,333
- [Mike] Say, quiet man.

300
00:12:55,800 --> 00:12:58,267
- Hey kid, is your
father at home?

301
00:12:58,267 --> 00:12:59,867
- I don't have a father.

302
00:12:59,867 --> 00:13:01,167
- Oh.

303
00:13:01,167 --> 00:13:02,433
Does the harbor
master live here?

304
00:13:02,433 --> 00:13:03,967
- It's the right place
you've come to, then.

305
00:13:03,967 --> 00:13:05,500
Come in.

306
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:06,767
- [Tom] Did you see me in
"How Green was my Valley?"

307
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:11,967
- [Crow] Me mum's at
work, so I'm having

308
00:13:11,967 --> 00:13:13,333
a garage sale with her stuff.

309
00:13:13,333 --> 00:13:15,433
- You say the harbor
master lives here?

310
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:18,567
- Yes, but it's
a city man he is.

311
00:13:18,567 --> 00:13:20,300
A government man.

312
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:21,100
- Who is he?

313
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:21,933
What's he do?

314
00:13:21,933 --> 00:13:22,867
- McCartin's his name.

315
00:13:22,867 --> 00:13:24,400
Calls himself an archeologist.

316
00:13:25,467 --> 00:13:26,733
- What's your name?

317
00:13:26,733 --> 00:13:28,067
- Shawn, I work for him.

318
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:30,533
- Doing what?

319
00:13:30,533 --> 00:13:32,967
- Oh, bits of this
and bits of that.

320
00:13:32,967 --> 00:13:34,567
Cleaning up the things he finds.

321
00:13:35,600 --> 00:13:37,000
- What things?

322
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:38,600
- You like to see them, then?

323
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:40,067
- Yeah, sure.

324
00:13:40,067 --> 00:13:42,500
- [Mike] You know, he looks
like a chimp that's dressed up.

325
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:48,200
(upbeat music)

326
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:53,833
- [Mike] Hey, look over
there, it's an ancient

327
00:13:53,833 --> 00:13:55,233
Celtic waffle iron.

328
00:13:55,233 --> 00:13:56,300
- [Tom] Cool.

329
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:59,133
- [Crow] Kid, how
does this huge room

330
00:13:59,133 --> 00:14:00,633
fit into this tiny little hut?

331
00:14:03,867 --> 00:14:05,000
- Well, what do you know?

332
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,433
- [Viewers] Not much, you?

333
00:14:10,267 --> 00:14:11,200
- [Shawn] That's
been under the sea

334
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:13,367
for a thousand years, they say.

335
00:14:13,367 --> 00:14:15,000
- Viking, looks like.

336
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:15,867
- Yeah?

337
00:14:15,867 --> 00:14:17,300
- That's it.

338
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:19,200
It was a great sea battle,
right off bay here.

339
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:20,433
- [Tom] Great.

340
00:14:20,433 --> 00:14:22,700
- They throwed them off
and sunk their ships.

341
00:14:22,700 --> 00:14:24,200
- What's that?

342
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:26,167
- [Tom] Oh, that's a taco
gaith bovine growth hormone.

343
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:30,633
- That's Olgra, the sea spirit.

344
00:14:30,633 --> 00:14:32,967
Just blind like he did that day.

345
00:14:34,100 --> 00:14:36,733
- Sounds like Saint
Patrick and the snakes.

346
00:14:36,733 --> 00:14:38,267
- What are you doing here?

347
00:14:38,267 --> 00:14:39,100
You get out.

348
00:14:39,100 --> 00:14:40,400
- [Mike] Samuel Beckett.

349
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:42,233
- [Tom] Not cool.

350
00:14:42,233 --> 00:14:43,600
- I'm Joe Ryan.

351
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:45,000
That's my partner, Sam Slade.

352
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:45,833
- Hi.

353
00:14:45,833 --> 00:14:47,933
- McCartin's the name.

354
00:14:47,933 --> 00:14:49,367
Salvage vessel, aren't ya?

355
00:14:49,367 --> 00:14:50,600
- Yeah.

356
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:51,433
- I thought so.

357
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:54,367
Did the boy tell
you about all this?

358
00:14:54,367 --> 00:14:55,733
- Yeah, a little.

359
00:14:55,733 --> 00:14:57,400
- This stuff has no real value,

360
00:14:57,400 --> 00:14:59,000
except the to our snake head.

361
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,033
But since it turned up,
no ships area allowed

362
00:15:01,033 --> 00:15:03,767
at that island for more than
24 hours without a permit.

363
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:06,633
- But friend, we're
not sea worthy.

364
00:15:06,633 --> 00:15:08,600
We won't be for
three or four days.

365
00:15:08,600 --> 00:15:11,000
- I don't make the orders,
that's the way it is.

366
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:13,633
- Anyway, we're afloat.

367
00:15:13,633 --> 00:15:14,867
Come on Sam.

368
00:15:14,867 --> 00:15:17,600
- [Crow] We'll go find
a better harbor master.

369
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:19,200
- Hey how about fresh water?

370
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:20,933
- We'll give you that.

371
00:15:20,933 --> 00:15:21,867
- Thanks.

372
00:15:21,867 --> 00:15:22,900
- No hard feelings, now.

373
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:24,367
- Oh no.

374
00:15:24,367 --> 00:15:26,000
- [Mike] Now wait, you guys
I think there might be.

375
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:27,600
Please, let's talk this out.

376
00:15:30,200 --> 00:15:32,300
- You believe him,
about this permit?

377
00:15:33,867 --> 00:15:34,867
- No.

378
00:15:34,867 --> 00:15:36,333
- Well, something's eating him.

379
00:15:36,333 --> 00:15:37,533
- [Tom] You mean
besides the lice?

380
00:15:37,533 --> 00:15:38,600
- I wonder what.

381
00:15:39,967 --> 00:15:40,800
- I don't know.

382
00:15:42,167 --> 00:15:43,667
Come on.

383
00:15:43,667 --> 00:15:45,400
Maybe we'll swing a little
more weight around here

384
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:46,867
if we find out.

385
00:15:46,867 --> 00:15:48,867
- [Crow] But let's get
some boiled cabbage first.

386
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:53,400
- [Mike] Ah, it's the
yearly silky clubbin'.

387
00:15:55,867 --> 00:15:57,267
- Hey Joe, look.

388
00:15:57,267 --> 00:15:58,300
- [Tom] He's new to sailoring,

389
00:15:58,300 --> 00:15:59,800
he's still pointing out boats.

390
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:04,733
- Smack her off, Harry.

391
00:16:04,733 --> 00:16:05,933
- [Crow] Beg your pardon?

392
00:16:07,433 --> 00:16:09,567
- [Mike] Oh, we're a
terrible crew team.

393
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:13,600
- Are you having any luck?

394
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:15,867
(speaking foreign language)

395
00:16:15,867 --> 00:16:17,133
- You know Joe, they
can talk English

396
00:16:17,133 --> 00:16:18,900
every bit as good
as you and I can.

397
00:16:18,900 --> 00:16:20,100
They just don't
want to, that's all.

398
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:22,067
- You try, Harry.

399
00:16:22,067 --> 00:16:25,967
(speaking in foreign language)

400
00:16:28,067 --> 00:16:29,900
- He said there's no time
for talking with strangers,

401
00:16:29,900 --> 00:16:32,133
and McCarton does all the
talkin' on the island.

402
00:16:32,133 --> 00:16:35,033
- [Tom] He said, blow it out
your something, I'm not sure.

403
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:38,900
Joe Strummer goes fishing.

404
00:16:40,433 --> 00:16:43,800
(speaking foreign language)

405
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:45,633
- Two of the divers
didn't come up, Joe.

406
00:16:45,633 --> 00:16:46,833
They think they're gone.

407
00:16:47,800 --> 00:16:49,333
- Gone?

408
00:16:49,333 --> 00:16:50,800
- [Crow] Yeah, it's a word
meaning not here anymore.

409
00:16:53,300 --> 00:16:55,633
- [Mike] Stop shaking
the boat, I'm gonna puke.

410
00:16:57,967 --> 00:17:01,367
(Servo speaking in
a foreign language)

411
00:17:01,367 --> 00:17:02,533
- There's one.

412
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:05,533
(speaking foreign language)

413
00:17:05,533 --> 00:17:08,667
- [Crow] He's got two
time release caplets
lets on his back.

414
00:17:08,667 --> 00:17:10,167
- [Tom] Now he's only got one.

415
00:17:13,667 --> 00:17:15,433
- [Mike] Overcome by
an excess of potatoes,

416
00:17:15,433 --> 00:17:16,700
Guinness, and guilt.

417
00:17:19,267 --> 00:17:21,333
- [Tom] Okay, let's take
a look at that butt.

418
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:29,567
- So that's what's
worrying McCartin.

419
00:17:29,567 --> 00:17:31,700
- [Mike] I should visit
Taneil when I'm ashore.

420
00:17:33,533 --> 00:17:35,133
- [Tom] The mackerel's in the...

421
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:40,233
- He's dead.

422
00:17:40,233 --> 00:17:41,167
- Dead how?

423
00:17:41,167 --> 00:17:42,000
- [Crow] How dead?

424
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:42,833
Well, just dead.

425
00:17:42,833 --> 00:17:43,933
- You saw him come up.

426
00:17:45,567 --> 00:17:47,767
You ask me, he died of fright.

427
00:17:47,767 --> 00:17:49,467
- [Tom] Couldn't be the
boiled back fat he ate

428
00:17:49,467 --> 00:17:50,800
everyday of his life.

429
00:17:54,300 --> 00:17:55,700
- [Mike] I think
that's our ship.

430
00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:57,500
It's just they
all look the same.

431
00:17:59,500 --> 00:18:00,367
- This is crazy.

432
00:18:00,367 --> 00:18:01,567
- [Tom] Us marrying.

433
00:18:01,567 --> 00:18:02,900
- There was a man
lost down there today.

434
00:18:02,900 --> 00:18:04,800
Not even a trace.

435
00:18:04,800 --> 00:18:07,733
Another one saw something
that scared him to death.

436
00:18:07,733 --> 00:18:09,233
- Look Sam, you
don't want to go?

437
00:18:09,233 --> 00:18:10,767
Don't go.

438
00:18:10,767 --> 00:18:12,767
- I'm going, all right.

439
00:18:12,767 --> 00:18:14,533
Just to make sure you
get back to shore.

440
00:18:14,533 --> 00:18:15,633
- [Crow] Oh, I got back.

441
00:18:15,633 --> 00:18:17,600
- Sure, with a hat full of gold.

442
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:19,567
- [Mike] Ha, you get it?

443
00:18:19,567 --> 00:18:20,400
- That's that.

444
00:18:22,467 --> 00:18:23,667
- Yeah, okay.

445
00:18:23,667 --> 00:18:24,667
- [Tom] How do
these things work?

446
00:18:29,433 --> 00:18:31,233
- [Crow] This one
smells like yours.

447
00:18:36,267 --> 00:18:38,300
- [Mike] Boat made of
foot-long hot dogs.

448
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,833
- [Crow] I'm just gonna hang
it out over the side here.

449
00:18:42,833 --> 00:18:43,667
Don't look.

450
00:18:44,867 --> 00:18:47,633
(water splashing)

451
00:18:47,633 --> 00:18:48,867
(suspenseful music)

452
00:18:48,867 --> 00:18:50,167
- [Tom] "Thunder Boar."

453
00:18:50,167 --> 00:18:52,200
- [Crow] "Voyage to the
Bottom of the Yawn."

454
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:53,733
- [Tom] "The Abysmal."

455
00:18:53,733 --> 00:18:55,733
- [Crow] "Driving Miss Dummy."

456
00:18:55,733 --> 00:18:58,000
- [Mike] Wait, that
doesn't really work at all.

457
00:18:58,000 --> 00:18:59,500
- [Crow] Really?

458
00:18:59,500 --> 00:19:01,467
- [Mike] No, it really fails
on a whole lot of levels.

459
00:19:01,467 --> 00:19:03,033
- [Crow] Well, I
try Mike, all right?

460
00:19:03,033 --> 00:19:03,867
- [Mike] Sorry.

461
00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:06,867
- [Tom] Watch for
sharks, I accidentally

462
00:19:06,867 --> 00:19:08,767
bathed in fish
blood this morning.

463
00:19:17,867 --> 00:19:21,200
Did you say (gurgling)?

464
00:19:24,267 --> 00:19:26,100
- [Crow] Wow, all
this hard water.

465
00:19:26,100 --> 00:19:27,667
Ireland needs the Culligan Man.

466
00:19:29,700 --> 00:19:31,800
- [Mike] Counting
plankton is really hard.

467
00:19:37,967 --> 00:19:39,600
- [Tom] Somebody sunk a
Macy's Thanksgiving Day

468
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:40,433
parade balloon.

469
00:19:43,467 --> 00:19:46,000
- [Mike] Come on, let's get
some stale bread and feed it.

470
00:19:48,067 --> 00:19:49,300
- [Crow] You know what's
great though, Sam?

471
00:19:49,300 --> 00:19:50,500
I'm wetting my pants in terror,

472
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:52,433
but it really doesn't
matter down here.

473
00:19:56,233 --> 00:19:57,500
- What did you see, Sam?

474
00:19:57,500 --> 00:19:59,833
- [Tom] Wanna borrow
my old dish towel?

475
00:19:59,833 --> 00:20:01,067
- I don't know.

476
00:20:02,467 --> 00:20:04,367
But whatever it was, I
never wanna see it again.

477
00:20:04,367 --> 00:20:06,267
- [Mike] Oh, like
your this big prize.

478
00:20:11,167 --> 00:20:14,567
♪ Show me the way to go home ♪

479
00:20:14,567 --> 00:20:16,333
♪ Ireland ♪

480
00:20:16,333 --> 00:20:18,533
- [Tom] There's a dead
hobbit in that thing.

481
00:20:20,867 --> 00:20:22,900
- How goes it, Sam?

482
00:20:22,900 --> 00:20:25,000
- These are the last few
barrels coming on board now.

483
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,833
- [Mike] Well clear the stage
for a campy musical review.

484
00:20:29,267 --> 00:20:31,233
- Keep back here, boy.

485
00:20:31,233 --> 00:20:32,433
- Captain wants to see ya.

486
00:20:32,433 --> 00:20:34,100
- [Crow] Thought
his name was McBox.

487
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:37,767
(speaking foreign language)

488
00:20:39,867 --> 00:20:41,867
- Look kid, get down
off there, will ya?

489
00:20:41,867 --> 00:20:43,800
- A science experiment.

490
00:20:43,800 --> 00:20:44,667
- What about the permit?

491
00:20:44,667 --> 00:20:46,233
- It's heathen lie.

492
00:20:46,233 --> 00:20:47,867
- Maybe we better see what
this joker's got in mind.

493
00:20:47,867 --> 00:20:49,100
Where is he?

494
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:50,067
- Come on, then.

495
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:52,067
- Harry.

496
00:20:52,067 --> 00:20:53,333
- [Tom] Well here's the script.

497
00:20:53,333 --> 00:20:55,000
You take my part in
the movie for a while.

498
00:20:58,867 --> 00:21:00,567
- [Mike] Their
Polynesian torch ceremony

499
00:21:00,567 --> 00:21:02,100
isn't too successful this year.

500
00:21:03,633 --> 00:21:04,867
- What's going on here?

501
00:21:04,867 --> 00:21:08,600
- They think a shark
got the man who is lost.

502
00:21:11,833 --> 00:21:14,667
♪ I will call you ♪

503
00:21:19,733 --> 00:21:22,367
- Mister McCartin, I
hear you got a beef.

504
00:21:22,367 --> 00:21:24,433
- You were snoopin'
around out there.

505
00:21:24,433 --> 00:21:26,200
My divers told me.

506
00:21:26,200 --> 00:21:27,667
- Yeah, and I suppose
they told you to give us

507
00:21:27,667 --> 00:21:28,867
all that jazz about a permit.

508
00:21:28,867 --> 00:21:31,200
- Are you doubting my word?

509
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:33,100
- You're right,
Mr. McCartin, I am.

510
00:21:33,100 --> 00:21:35,700
- [Crow] Look, I'm trying
to write Crap's last tape.

511
00:21:38,367 --> 00:21:41,367
- [Mike] May your corn
beef not pickle correctly.

512
00:21:41,367 --> 00:21:43,967
- After you're loaded
with water, you leave.

513
00:21:43,967 --> 00:21:45,533
Tonight.

514
00:21:45,533 --> 00:21:46,733
- The sooner the better.

515
00:21:46,733 --> 00:21:47,600
- [Tom] The tighter the sweater.

516
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:48,967
The boys depend on us.

517
00:21:48,967 --> 00:21:49,767
- [Crow] Yay.

518
00:21:51,400 --> 00:21:53,467
There's like one cod left
in the North Atlantic,

519
00:21:53,467 --> 00:21:55,067
but dammit, they're
gonna get it.

520
00:22:01,500 --> 00:22:02,767
(speaking foreign language)

521
00:22:02,767 --> 00:22:04,200
- [Tom] No, it's just
Grandma taking a swim.

522
00:22:04,200 --> 00:22:05,033
Don't!

523
00:22:11,467 --> 00:22:12,800
- [Crow] Oh, ow.

524
00:22:16,767 --> 00:22:19,967
- [Mike] Patty cake, patty cake.

525
00:22:19,967 --> 00:22:21,833
- [Crow] Hey, go
check that out, kid.

526
00:22:25,333 --> 00:22:26,233
- Father.

527
00:22:26,233 --> 00:22:27,167
- [Mike] How are ya?

528
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,500
- [Tom] It's the rare
isosceles underwater dragon.

529
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:38,033
- [Crow] Really, gentlemen,
must we do this every night?

530
00:22:39,933 --> 00:22:42,067
- [Mike] Oh, poor Robert
Shaw, eaten again.

531
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,267
♪ To reach ♪

532
00:22:53,800 --> 00:22:57,533
(doors creaking and closing)

533
00:23:05,933 --> 00:23:08,367
- Oh hello, I'm Diana Ring.

534
00:23:08,367 --> 00:23:10,667
Inspired by the guy
in today's movie

535
00:23:10,667 --> 00:23:13,100
who looks kind of
like Samuel Beckett,

536
00:23:13,100 --> 00:23:16,700
we present to you a
tragic comedy in one act.

537
00:23:16,700 --> 00:23:18,867
Waiting for Gorgo.

538
00:23:18,867 --> 00:23:20,167
Thank you, won't we?

539
00:23:28,967 --> 00:23:30,233
- Nothing to be done.

540
00:23:31,433 --> 00:23:33,667
- I'm beginning to come
around to that opinion.

541
00:23:36,167 --> 00:23:37,500
- Let's go.

542
00:23:37,500 --> 00:23:38,833
- We can't.

543
00:23:38,833 --> 00:23:39,900
- Why not?

544
00:23:39,900 --> 00:23:42,933
- We're waiting for Gorgo.

545
00:23:42,933 --> 00:23:44,167
- Ah, yes.

546
00:23:44,167 --> 00:23:45,833
(growling and shouting)

547
00:23:45,833 --> 00:23:47,333
- And there he is!

548
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:51,167
- No no no no.

549
00:23:57,933 --> 00:24:01,233
(clapping and cheering)

550
00:24:55,267 --> 00:24:57,233
- We'll be back
after this message

551
00:24:57,233 --> 00:24:59,500
for whiskey and cigarettes.

552
00:25:01,367 --> 00:25:03,967
(upbeat music)

553
00:25:08,467 --> 00:25:09,900
- [Tom] Oh, that was fun.

554
00:25:09,900 --> 00:25:11,567
- [Crow] Whiskey and cigarettes.

555
00:25:11,567 --> 00:25:12,967
- [Mike] Look at this.

556
00:25:12,967 --> 00:25:15,033
Poor Irish, ya know, if
they're not invaded by Cromwell

557
00:25:15,033 --> 00:25:17,667
or infested with leprechauns,
they got this guy.

558
00:25:21,200 --> 00:25:22,900
- [Tom] I'll get me slingshot.

559
00:25:22,900 --> 00:25:24,167
- Shoot!

560
00:25:24,167 --> 00:25:27,167
(gunshots banging)

561
00:25:27,167 --> 00:25:30,833
♪ Shuffle off to the fellow ♪

562
00:25:32,167 --> 00:25:34,367
- [Mike] McRoar, O'Growl!

563
00:25:34,367 --> 00:25:37,067
(suspenseful music)

564
00:25:37,067 --> 00:25:38,267
- [Tom] Sorry,
I'm a little lost.

565
00:25:38,267 --> 00:25:39,800
Can you tell me how
to get to Tokyo?

566
00:25:43,600 --> 00:25:45,167
- [Mike] Quick, let's emigrate!

567
00:25:52,300 --> 00:25:55,000
- [Tom] This guy's good at
pushing kids around, ya know.

568
00:25:56,767 --> 00:25:59,433
(Gorgo roaring)

569
00:26:01,033 --> 00:26:03,367
- [Mike] Mmm habenero sauce.

570
00:26:05,333 --> 00:26:07,200
- [Crow] Not a very
good juggling act.

571
00:26:09,167 --> 00:26:10,633
- [Mike] A real
shame for the island.

572
00:26:10,633 --> 00:26:12,900
The Let's Go Europe people
are visiting tonight.

573
00:26:15,767 --> 00:26:18,333
- [Tom] These walking
catfish are really big, whew.

574
00:26:21,967 --> 00:26:22,967
- [Crow] Dave Foley.

575
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:28,233
- [Tom] Come on, I was
just coming to the kallie.

576
00:26:28,233 --> 00:26:29,833
I guess you don't want me, then.

577
00:26:36,667 --> 00:26:39,667
- [Mike] I'll put on my asbestos
underwear and I'll be back!

578
00:26:41,167 --> 00:26:43,467
- [Crow] Fine, I'll
spend my Euros elsewhere.

579
00:26:45,467 --> 00:26:50,467
- [Mike] Well I never.

580
00:26:52,367 --> 00:26:54,733
- [Tom] Open the pub,
come on it's nine a.m.!

581
00:26:58,400 --> 00:27:00,900
- [Crow] We all
hated "End Game."

582
00:27:00,900 --> 00:27:03,167
- Now there's a man looks
like he's got a real problem.

583
00:27:03,167 --> 00:27:04,000
- Uh-huh.

584
00:27:05,167 --> 00:27:06,200
I'll bet he'd pay
through the nose

585
00:27:06,200 --> 00:27:07,600
if we could solve it for him.

586
00:27:07,600 --> 00:27:09,900
- [Mike] I said we're
all out of Lucky Charms.

587
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:14,233
- Hey, hey, mister.

588
00:27:14,233 --> 00:27:15,900
We'll be wanting a
passage on your ship

589
00:27:15,900 --> 00:27:19,200
just as soon as he gets
around to giving us our pay.

590
00:27:19,200 --> 00:27:20,767
- Now hold on.

591
00:27:20,767 --> 00:27:22,800
You'll get your pay if you like.

592
00:27:22,800 --> 00:27:24,700
But there'll be no taking
a passage on any ship.

593
00:27:24,700 --> 00:27:25,767
- Why not?

594
00:27:25,767 --> 00:27:27,333
(crowd shouting)

595
00:27:27,333 --> 00:27:29,367
- [Crow] I think
they're mumbling a
very good point there.

596
00:27:29,367 --> 00:27:32,100
- Now for god's sake, let's
go inside and talk about it.

597
00:27:32,100 --> 00:27:33,600
- Sure.

598
00:27:33,600 --> 00:27:35,267
- [Tom] He could be a
Baldwin brother, couldn't he?

599
00:27:37,333 --> 00:27:38,800
- If they all leave now,
my portmen, my divers,

600
00:27:38,800 --> 00:27:41,167
the lot of them, then the
whole thing will go to smash.

601
00:27:41,167 --> 00:27:43,000
- Hey, I kinda see their point.

602
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:45,000
- Got any other reason for
not wanting them to leave?

603
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:46,267
- What do you mean?

604
00:27:46,267 --> 00:27:49,400
- Oh, like, maybe
they talk to much.

605
00:27:49,400 --> 00:27:51,100
- [Mike] About these
laundry tokens.

606
00:27:51,100 --> 00:27:52,033
- Where did you get those?

607
00:27:52,033 --> 00:27:53,567
- Same place as you.

608
00:27:53,567 --> 00:27:56,067
Supposing we got rid
of that thing for ya.

609
00:27:56,067 --> 00:27:57,267
- The beast?

610
00:27:57,267 --> 00:27:58,600
- [Crow] That's
right, Ian Paisley.

611
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:00,367
- Do you think you can?

612
00:28:00,367 --> 00:28:01,867
- Maybe.

613
00:28:01,867 --> 00:28:03,533
- [Tom] If you give me one
of your non-sexual massages.

614
00:28:03,533 --> 00:28:05,567
- Let's take another
look in there fast, huh?

615
00:28:05,567 --> 00:28:08,033
- [Mike] I wanna play with
your Hot Wheels again.

616
00:28:14,767 --> 00:28:16,000
- [Tom] Sorry about
how this looks,

617
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:17,533
the walls need to be re-dunged.

618
00:28:18,700 --> 00:28:19,533
- Open the safe.

619
00:28:20,733 --> 00:28:22,600
- [Crow] Man, I'm
the harbor master.

620
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:24,933
I don't have to
listen to you guys.

621
00:28:24,933 --> 00:28:27,033
I can go master the
harbor anytime I want.

622
00:28:27,033 --> 00:28:29,000
This is so bogus.

623
00:28:33,400 --> 00:28:35,100
- [Voiceover] You've done
all right for yourself.

624
00:28:35,100 --> 00:28:36,733
- [Tom] Eva.

625
00:28:36,733 --> 00:28:37,667
- How about this
for a down payment?

626
00:28:37,667 --> 00:28:38,900
Do you like this one, Sam?

627
00:28:39,800 --> 00:28:41,233
- You must be out of your mind.

628
00:28:41,233 --> 00:28:42,467
A thing like that is priceless.

629
00:28:42,467 --> 00:28:43,900
- [Mike] It belonged
to Hagar the horrible.

630
00:28:43,900 --> 00:28:46,300
- And how do you think
you're gonna dispose of it?

631
00:28:46,300 --> 00:28:48,733
- You must've figured out a way.

632
00:28:48,733 --> 00:28:49,567
I bet we can.

633
00:28:50,467 --> 00:28:52,633
(shouting indistinctly)

634
00:28:52,633 --> 00:28:54,533
You're getting off easy, friend.

635
00:28:54,533 --> 00:28:56,233
Could've grabbed the lot.

636
00:28:56,233 --> 00:28:57,867
- When will you start?

637
00:28:57,867 --> 00:28:59,000
- Now.

638
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:00,467
Why not?

639
00:29:00,467 --> 00:29:02,500
- [Mike] Heh, totally toasted
your Irish soda bread.

640
00:29:09,200 --> 00:29:10,067
- Mr. Ryan!

641
00:29:10,067 --> 00:29:10,900
- [Tom] How's your hope?

642
00:29:10,900 --> 00:29:11,833
- Mr. Ryan!

643
00:29:11,833 --> 00:29:12,667
- [Tom] And your daughter?

644
00:29:12,667 --> 00:29:14,033
- What is it, kid?

645
00:29:14,033 --> 00:29:15,900
- You'll be trying
to catch him, Gorgo.

646
00:29:15,900 --> 00:29:16,733
- Yeah, maybe.

647
00:29:17,667 --> 00:29:18,933
Not afraid, are ya?

648
00:29:18,933 --> 00:29:21,400
- No, but it's a bad
thing you're doing.

649
00:29:21,400 --> 00:29:23,200
A terrible bad thing, Mr. Ryan.

650
00:29:24,367 --> 00:29:26,333
- Why don't you call me Joe?

651
00:29:26,333 --> 00:29:27,333
- Okay, Joe.

652
00:29:28,733 --> 00:29:30,233
- Okay, Shawn.

653
00:29:32,033 --> 00:29:33,167
- [Tom] But you missed my point,

654
00:29:33,167 --> 00:29:35,967
despite our new
familiarity with names.

655
00:29:35,967 --> 00:29:37,033
- Might be able to
catch him in the nets.

656
00:29:37,033 --> 00:29:38,133
- [Crow] Who, the kid?

657
00:29:38,133 --> 00:29:39,400
- Yeah.

658
00:29:39,400 --> 00:29:40,733
- How do you suppose to
kill a thing like that?

659
00:29:40,733 --> 00:29:42,367
- Oh, I don't know.

660
00:29:42,367 --> 00:29:43,300
Dynamite, maybe.

661
00:29:44,633 --> 00:29:46,233
- Ever stop to think
what a thing like that

662
00:29:46,233 --> 00:29:47,700
might be worth alive?

663
00:29:47,700 --> 00:29:50,200
- [Tom] Probably buy a
hundred roll neck sweaters.

664
00:29:56,767 --> 00:29:57,833
- [Mike] Bought it
from the government.

665
00:29:57,833 --> 00:29:59,433
It's called Fatman or something.

666
00:30:03,033 --> 00:30:05,433
- You really think you'll
catch him with that?

667
00:30:05,433 --> 00:30:07,167
- Sure, why not?

668
00:30:07,167 --> 00:30:09,733
I think it'll be a light
selector sort of lure.

669
00:30:09,733 --> 00:30:11,933
If that doesn't work,
we'll try something else.

670
00:30:11,933 --> 00:30:13,300
Okay Harry, come on, okay.

671
00:30:13,300 --> 00:30:15,200
- [Tom] Try something
else, brilliant.

672
00:30:17,633 --> 00:30:19,133
- Johnny, stop!

673
00:30:19,133 --> 00:30:21,933
- [Crow] There's a skeleton of
a little French guy in here.

674
00:30:26,700 --> 00:30:28,367
- Ready when you are, Joe.

675
00:30:28,367 --> 00:30:29,867
- Okay.

676
00:30:29,867 --> 00:30:30,867
Take her up!

677
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:33,600
- [Tom] Wait wait, what
are you doing to me?

678
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:35,267
- Take her out, out.

679
00:30:36,500 --> 00:30:37,667
Lower away!

680
00:30:39,333 --> 00:30:40,600
Down!

681
00:30:40,600 --> 00:30:41,900
- [Mike] Cast him over
by those lily pads.

682
00:30:43,133 --> 00:30:46,000
(mysterious music)

683
00:30:47,733 --> 00:30:50,067
- [Crow] Well, I'd sure hate
to go underwater in this thing.

684
00:30:50,067 --> 00:30:50,867
Guys?

685
00:30:52,067 --> 00:30:53,333
- [Tom] He immediately
crossed lines

686
00:30:53,333 --> 00:30:55,033
with the guy trying
to catch Godzilla.

687
00:30:58,967 --> 00:31:00,133
- [Mike] Let's roll
down the window here.

688
00:31:00,133 --> 00:31:00,867
- [Tom] D'oh!

689
00:31:02,200 --> 00:31:03,133
- Tom?

690
00:31:03,133 --> 00:31:03,933
- [Crow] Can I come up now?

691
00:31:03,933 --> 00:31:04,733
- Can you hear me?

692
00:31:04,733 --> 00:31:06,200
- Yeah, I can hear ya.

693
00:31:06,200 --> 00:31:07,600
- Give me about 15 fathoms.

694
00:31:07,600 --> 00:31:08,600
- Down!

695
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:10,033
- [Tom] Would you let me finish?

696
00:31:12,033 --> 00:31:14,700
- [Mike] Yeah, Civil War
submarine technology.

697
00:31:17,100 --> 00:31:19,200
- [Tom] Why does that
octopus have a hack saw?

698
00:31:27,167 --> 00:31:28,400
- Tom?

699
00:31:28,400 --> 00:31:29,267
- [Crow] I mean it,
I'm really scared now.

700
00:31:29,267 --> 00:31:30,367
- Tom?

701
00:31:30,367 --> 00:31:32,233
- 10 fathoms, how's that?

702
00:31:32,233 --> 00:31:33,900
- Okay, that'll do.

703
00:31:34,767 --> 00:31:36,733
Give me four to five knots.

704
00:31:36,733 --> 00:31:38,900
And try to head down the bay.

705
00:31:38,900 --> 00:31:40,333
- Slow engine!

706
00:31:40,333 --> 00:31:41,633
- [Mike] Shark.

707
00:31:41,633 --> 00:31:42,467
Shark.

708
00:31:46,967 --> 00:31:49,967
- [Tom] A little mood
lighting. (chuckling)

709
00:31:53,833 --> 00:31:56,367
(upbeat music)

710
00:31:59,967 --> 00:32:01,300
- [Crow] Oh, if they don't
let me up to go soon,

711
00:32:01,300 --> 00:32:03,167
it will be a yellow submarine.

712
00:32:03,167 --> 00:32:06,100
(mysterious music)

713
00:32:10,033 --> 00:32:11,400
- [Tom] They drag
him into a rock,

714
00:32:11,400 --> 00:32:13,200
'cause they're so
delighted with the red sky.

715
00:32:15,400 --> 00:32:16,500
- [Mike] Steven Segal.

716
00:32:22,600 --> 00:32:23,900
- Tom, can you hear me?

717
00:32:23,900 --> 00:32:25,600
Stop the engines!

718
00:32:25,600 --> 00:32:27,133
- Stop engines!

719
00:32:27,133 --> 00:32:29,000
- [Crow] Mm, canned guy.

720
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:30,667
- 10 fathoms, not to fast,
I don't want to lose him.

721
00:32:30,667 --> 00:32:32,567
- Slow up.

722
00:32:32,567 --> 00:32:33,400
Up, up.

723
00:32:35,533 --> 00:32:37,067
- [Tom] What your laundry sees.

724
00:32:43,600 --> 00:32:46,233
- [Mike] Did we
develop a step two yet?

725
00:32:46,233 --> 00:32:47,067
- Tom!

726
00:32:47,067 --> 00:32:48,000
Tom, pull up!

727
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:50,167
- Up, quick, quick, pull up, up!

728
00:32:50,167 --> 00:32:52,000
- [Crow] Gee,
yeoman pushy pants.

729
00:32:54,367 --> 00:32:56,300
- Tom, Tom, pull up!

730
00:32:56,300 --> 00:32:57,600
- Pull up!
- Arr.

731
00:32:58,500 --> 00:32:59,733
Mm, yummy.

732
00:32:59,733 --> 00:33:00,567
- Tom!

733
00:33:00,567 --> 00:33:01,567
Tom!

734
00:33:01,567 --> 00:33:02,400
Pull up.

735
00:33:03,433 --> 00:33:04,633
- [Mike] Arr.

736
00:33:04,633 --> 00:33:06,233
- Tom, quick!
- Arr!

737
00:33:07,767 --> 00:33:09,100
- Tom, pull out!

738
00:33:10,267 --> 00:33:12,167
- It won't take anymore,
she'll snap the cable!

739
00:33:12,167 --> 00:33:13,300
- Drop the nets!

740
00:33:13,300 --> 00:33:14,433
Drop the nets!

741
00:33:14,433 --> 00:33:15,633
- Drop the nets!

742
00:33:15,633 --> 00:33:16,733
All hands on deck!

743
00:33:16,733 --> 00:33:19,133
(dramatic music)

744
00:33:19,133 --> 00:33:21,200
- [Crow] Hey there's no pop top!

745
00:33:23,200 --> 00:33:24,300
- [Man] Drop it.

746
00:33:24,300 --> 00:33:25,133
- [Man] Drop it!

747
00:33:26,300 --> 00:33:27,567
- [Mike] Now he's
laying his eggs.

748
00:33:27,567 --> 00:33:28,733
He must expect me
to swim over 'em.

749
00:33:34,567 --> 00:33:35,867
- [Crow] So did Gorgo cooperate

750
00:33:35,867 --> 00:33:38,200
and just wrap
himself in the net?

751
00:33:38,200 --> 00:33:40,000
- [Mike] How did this happen?

752
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:41,067
- [Tom] Dumb.

753
00:33:44,367 --> 00:33:45,600
- [Man] Heavy on the winds!

754
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:46,733
- [Mike] Light on the starch!

755
00:33:46,733 --> 00:33:48,400
- [Crow] Easy on the mayonnaise!

756
00:33:50,567 --> 00:33:53,167
- [Tom] They're gonna have
a big Gorgo bowl tonight.

757
00:33:55,200 --> 00:33:56,467
- Bring him in!

758
00:33:57,867 --> 00:33:58,733
Lower away!

759
00:34:00,267 --> 00:34:01,500
Tighten up those nets!

760
00:34:01,500 --> 00:34:03,867
- [Mike] Brian just fell
into one of his pores!

761
00:34:08,567 --> 00:34:12,200
- [Crow] Am in Ireland,
send real food.

762
00:34:13,500 --> 00:34:14,800
- [Tom] Hello Mister
and Miss Northumbria

763
00:34:14,800 --> 00:34:16,300
and all the ships at sea.

764
00:34:16,300 --> 00:34:18,700
- The headlines of the entire
world are being monopolized

765
00:34:18,700 --> 00:34:21,600
by the news of the capture
of a fantastic monster,

766
00:34:21,600 --> 00:34:25,800
seemingly of prehistoric origin,
off the coast of Ireland.

767
00:34:25,800 --> 00:34:27,467
Puzzled scientists
are already spectating

768
00:34:27,467 --> 00:34:29,667
that the monster may
have been released

769
00:34:29,667 --> 00:34:32,367
from some vast oceanic cavern,

770
00:34:32,367 --> 00:34:33,600
far beneath the Earth's crust,

771
00:34:33,600 --> 00:34:35,500
by unprecedented
volcanic eruptions

772
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:37,733
which occurred in
the area last week.

773
00:34:37,733 --> 00:34:40,000
Some scientific
authorities are suggesting

774
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:42,900
that the whole thing is merely
an elaborate Irish hoax.

775
00:34:42,900 --> 00:34:44,900
Nevertheless, the Irish
government is sending

776
00:34:44,900 --> 00:34:46,667
two of their top paleontologists

777
00:34:46,667 --> 00:34:48,667
to claim the
creature for Ireland.

778
00:34:48,667 --> 00:34:49,900
If it does exist.

779
00:34:51,100 --> 00:34:52,033
- [Crow] Oh they
set up the phone

780
00:34:52,033 --> 00:34:53,400
so he can talk to his family.

781
00:34:53,400 --> 00:34:55,767
- [Mike] Hi honey, got
captured again, yeah.

782
00:34:55,767 --> 00:34:57,933
- There's your
friend, Professor.

783
00:34:57,933 --> 00:34:59,367
- I'll tell my
partner you're here.

784
00:35:01,900 --> 00:35:02,767
Radio, Skipper.

785
00:35:04,867 --> 00:35:06,200
- [Crow] London
turtle neck company

786
00:35:06,200 --> 00:35:09,000
has issued a recall of
potentially fatal flaw.

787
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:10,500
Oh, great.

788
00:35:10,500 --> 00:35:12,100
- Those two professors from
Dublin just come on board.

789
00:35:12,100 --> 00:35:12,933
- Show 'em up.

790
00:35:14,600 --> 00:35:15,400
- [Tom] I gotta go, honey.

791
00:35:15,400 --> 00:35:16,333
No, I'm fine.

792
00:35:16,333 --> 00:35:17,733
I gotta go, there's some guys.

793
00:35:18,833 --> 00:35:20,567
- I wonder if you
realize, Captain Randy,

794
00:35:20,567 --> 00:35:23,700
the enormous scientific
value of this discovery.

795
00:35:23,700 --> 00:35:25,400
- I think we do, Professor.

796
00:35:25,400 --> 00:35:28,167
- Well then, I shall wire
this to University of Dublin

797
00:35:28,167 --> 00:35:31,133
in order to make preparations
to receive the animal.

798
00:35:31,133 --> 00:35:33,033
You will proceed to Dublin.

799
00:35:33,033 --> 00:35:34,500
- [Mike] Who jiffed?

800
00:35:34,500 --> 00:35:36,433
- Where you'll be properly
compensated for your services.

801
00:35:36,433 --> 00:35:37,667
- Oh, sure, sure.

802
00:35:38,733 --> 00:35:41,433
- Well, we'll meet there.

803
00:35:41,433 --> 00:35:43,600
Unless you would like
one of us to go with you.

804
00:35:43,600 --> 00:35:45,467
- Oh, that won't be
necessary, Professor.

805
00:35:45,467 --> 00:35:47,400
- [Crow] I won't eat much.

806
00:35:47,400 --> 00:35:48,900
- Oh, there's one thing.

807
00:35:48,900 --> 00:35:51,600
The animal's skin
should be kept wet

808
00:35:51,600 --> 00:35:53,533
with a continuous
stream of water.

809
00:35:53,533 --> 00:35:54,467
- I see.

810
00:35:54,467 --> 00:35:55,333
- This is most important.

811
00:35:55,333 --> 00:35:56,567
- I'll take care of it.

812
00:35:56,567 --> 00:35:57,767
- When do you intend
sailing, Captain?

813
00:35:57,767 --> 00:35:59,367
- Tonight, if
that's okay by you.

814
00:35:59,367 --> 00:36:00,267
- Sooner the better.

815
00:36:00,267 --> 00:36:01,367
See you in Dublin.

816
00:36:01,367 --> 00:36:03,000
- University of Dublin.

817
00:36:03,000 --> 00:36:04,300
Properly compensated.

818
00:36:04,300 --> 00:36:05,667
- [Tom] Sentence fragments.

819
00:36:05,667 --> 00:36:07,267
- Big deal.

820
00:36:07,267 --> 00:36:08,467
- Take a look at this, Sam.

821
00:36:08,467 --> 00:36:10,367
- [Tom] Take a
look at this, Sam.

822
00:36:10,367 --> 00:36:12,533
- It's from Jalk
and Sax in London.

823
00:36:12,533 --> 00:36:15,500
They offer 30.000
against 50% of the gross.

824
00:36:15,500 --> 00:36:17,500
- [Crow] 50% of
Mary Gross, I mean.

825
00:36:19,233 --> 00:36:20,600
- Set a course for London, Sam.

826
00:36:20,600 --> 00:36:22,633
- [Tom] I got a
hankerin' for organ meat.

827
00:36:25,100 --> 00:36:26,300
- [Mike] Forgot to
bring in the stringer.

828
00:36:26,300 --> 00:36:27,867
Gorgo's draggin'.

829
00:36:27,867 --> 00:36:30,133
(light music)

830
00:36:30,133 --> 00:36:32,000
- [Crow] Soon Gorgo's
scampering around the deck,

831
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:33,833
making friends with
all the sailors.

832
00:36:37,733 --> 00:36:38,767
- [Tom] Oh, great.

833
00:36:43,433 --> 00:36:45,033
(ship horn blowing)

834
00:36:45,033 --> 00:36:46,733
- [Mike] Whoa,
that was me, sorry.

835
00:36:47,733 --> 00:36:48,833
(ship horn blowing)
Whoops.

836
00:36:52,300 --> 00:36:55,033
(Tom vocalizing)

837
00:36:57,800 --> 00:37:00,333
- [Crow] Kid, could you salt
yourself and come over here?

838
00:37:00,333 --> 00:37:02,633
- I came to let you
go back to the sea.

839
00:37:02,633 --> 00:37:04,600
- [Crow] Or maybe
lay in some marinade.

840
00:37:07,200 --> 00:37:10,200
- [Mike] I think Gorgo just
needs some new points and plugs.

841
00:37:13,100 --> 00:37:15,833
- [Crow] Go play on Gamra's
back, kid, I'm not into it.

842
00:37:21,933 --> 00:37:24,333
- You little knot head!

843
00:37:24,333 --> 00:37:25,500
What do you think you're doing?!

844
00:37:25,500 --> 00:37:26,333
- Take it easy.

845
00:37:27,800 --> 00:37:30,067
- I came to let him go back
to the sea where he belongs.

846
00:37:30,067 --> 00:37:31,333
- Why?

847
00:37:31,333 --> 00:37:33,067
- Maybe to save your
silly skins for ya.

848
00:37:33,067 --> 00:37:36,200
- I got a good notion to
toss you over the side.

849
00:37:37,367 --> 00:37:39,833
- You wouldn't be
sending me back now.

850
00:37:39,833 --> 00:37:41,533
- Harry.
- Here.

851
00:37:41,533 --> 00:37:43,800
- Better get our little
stowaway into a bunk.

852
00:37:43,800 --> 00:37:45,500
And wash his face.

853
00:37:45,500 --> 00:37:47,467
Harry, put a guard on
that thing 24 hours

854
00:37:47,467 --> 00:37:48,733
and give him a rifle.

855
00:37:48,733 --> 00:37:50,000
- [Crow] Don't give
the monster a rifle.

856
00:37:50,000 --> 00:37:51,700
- I could be watching
him for you, ya know.

857
00:37:51,700 --> 00:37:54,767
Yeah, you, you'd be
great, wouldn't ya?

858
00:37:54,767 --> 00:37:58,300
- Now if it so much as
moves, start shooting,

859
00:37:58,300 --> 00:38:00,500
and then run like the devil.

860
00:38:00,500 --> 00:38:02,367
- [Mike] Pick up my tail
and clatter across the deck

861
00:38:02,367 --> 00:38:04,500
with my cloven hooves?

862
00:38:04,500 --> 00:38:05,833
All right.

863
00:38:05,833 --> 00:38:07,500
- [Tom] Hey, screw,
just reach in my pocket,

864
00:38:07,500 --> 00:38:08,500
get me a cigarette.

865
00:38:10,933 --> 00:38:12,167
- Tell us something, kid.

866
00:38:13,400 --> 00:38:14,800
Anyone ever see
that thing before?

867
00:38:14,800 --> 00:38:17,000
- [Mike] Brandon Bean
did, but he was drunk.

868
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:19,533
- Why should anybody have to
see it to know it's there?

869
00:38:19,533 --> 00:38:22,933
- [Crow] Is this kid
speaking in Bam-bam language?

870
00:38:22,933 --> 00:38:24,367
- Go back to sleep, kid.

871
00:38:27,333 --> 00:38:29,000
- [Mike] We'll be turning
you over to Interpol.

872
00:38:29,000 --> 00:38:29,800
Good night.

873
00:38:32,900 --> 00:38:34,600
- [Crow] Let's go swipe
the captain's strawberries.

874
00:38:34,600 --> 00:38:35,567
That's always fun, eh?

875
00:38:35,567 --> 00:38:38,567
(ship horn blowing)

876
00:38:39,700 --> 00:38:42,567
♪ Average sized
bubbles in the brine ♪

877
00:38:42,567 --> 00:38:43,767
- Just phosphorus, Sam.

878
00:38:44,867 --> 00:38:46,933
- [Sam] But it's coming
from the scuffers.

879
00:38:46,933 --> 00:38:49,300
- It's the water off the animal.

880
00:38:49,300 --> 00:38:50,667
- So it's still salt water.

881
00:38:52,400 --> 00:38:53,933
- You seeing spirits too?

882
00:38:53,933 --> 00:38:55,433
(man screaming)

883
00:38:55,433 --> 00:38:58,100
- [Tom] Someone didn't rinse
out the sink after he shaved!

884
00:38:58,100 --> 00:38:58,933
- Crew!

885
00:38:58,933 --> 00:39:00,400
The decks, quick!

886
00:39:02,500 --> 00:39:05,333
- [Crow] Gorgo seduced the guard
and then tried to kill him.

887
00:39:06,800 --> 00:39:07,633
- Come on!

888
00:39:09,033 --> 00:39:11,967
- [Tom] I was having an
anti-scurvy lime, but fine.

889
00:39:14,733 --> 00:39:16,733
- [Mike] Oh no, it's
the cute one, d'oh.

890
00:39:20,467 --> 00:39:21,967
- Get him ready for bail, Sam.

891
00:39:24,867 --> 00:39:26,733
Kid, I told you to stay below.

892
00:39:26,733 --> 00:39:29,100
- [Crow] Okay, I'll go
up and stay below, then.

893
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:31,900
- [Tom] Hot-head.

894
00:39:33,800 --> 00:39:36,400
(upbeat music)

895
00:39:39,933 --> 00:39:40,767
- Filled with butter?

896
00:39:40,767 --> 00:39:41,800
- Guys, look at this.

897
00:39:41,800 --> 00:39:43,233
Guess what I got.

898
00:39:43,233 --> 00:39:45,267
The William Sylvester
version of Trivial Pursuit.

899
00:39:45,267 --> 00:39:47,600
- William Sylvester, hmm?

900
00:39:47,600 --> 00:39:49,900
- Yeah, yeah, I was gonna
get the baby boomer version

901
00:39:49,900 --> 00:39:52,333
of the William Sylvester
version of Trivial Pursuit,

902
00:39:52,333 --> 00:39:53,800
but I just thought the
basic version would be,

903
00:39:53,800 --> 00:39:55,433
you know, a lot more fun.

904
00:39:55,433 --> 00:39:56,900
What color do you want?

905
00:39:56,900 --> 00:39:58,267
- Well, let me answer
that, Mike, by asking you

906
00:39:58,267 --> 00:40:00,467
who the hell is
William Sylvester?

907
00:40:00,467 --> 00:40:03,333
- Come on, he's the
guy in today's movie.

908
00:40:03,333 --> 00:40:07,133
- I'm not recalling any
guy in today's movie.

909
00:40:07,133 --> 00:40:09,300
- Oh come on, he was the
main guy in today's movie.

910
00:40:09,300 --> 00:40:12,200
Plus he was in "Riding with
Death," and "Devil Doll,"

911
00:40:12,200 --> 00:40:15,300
plus "2001: A Space Odyssey,"
plus a whole bunch of other...

912
00:40:16,467 --> 00:40:18,600
Oh, oh, I see what
you guy's are doing.

913
00:40:18,600 --> 00:40:21,500
I see, you're trying to learn
all about William Sylvester

914
00:40:21,500 --> 00:40:23,900
so you can beat me at the
William Sylvester version

915
00:40:23,900 --> 00:40:24,900
of Trivial Pursuit.

916
00:40:24,900 --> 00:40:25,833
Well, it ain't gonna happen!

917
00:40:25,833 --> 00:40:26,900
You can go first though, Crow.

918
00:40:26,900 --> 00:40:28,467
- I don't want to.

919
00:40:28,467 --> 00:40:29,733
- Here you go.

920
00:40:29,733 --> 00:40:33,067
Okay, you have history
of William Sylvester.

921
00:40:34,567 --> 00:40:39,467
Okay, oh, oh man,
this is so easy.

922
00:40:39,467 --> 00:40:42,533
In "2001: A Space
Odyssey," what character

923
00:40:42,533 --> 00:40:44,467
did William Sylvester play?

924
00:40:44,467 --> 00:40:46,333
- I don't know.

925
00:40:46,333 --> 00:40:47,300
- What do you mean you don't?

926
00:40:47,300 --> 00:40:48,967
Haywood Floyd, Haywood Floyd.

927
00:40:48,967 --> 00:40:50,767
Everybody knows that.
- Haywood Floyd.

928
00:40:50,767 --> 00:40:52,467
- Okay, fine, your turn, Servo.

929
00:40:52,467 --> 00:40:53,733
- Oh goody.

930
00:40:53,733 --> 00:40:55,033
Okay, here we go.

931
00:40:55,967 --> 00:40:58,733
- Oh man, this is incredible.

932
00:40:58,733 --> 00:41:02,800
In the movie "Gorgo," who
played the character of Sam?

933
00:41:02,800 --> 00:41:04,567
- I don't know.

934
00:41:04,567 --> 00:41:05,500
- What do you mean?

935
00:41:05,500 --> 00:41:06,967
It was William Sylvester!

936
00:41:06,967 --> 00:41:08,600
It was William, we're
watching the movie today.

937
00:41:08,600 --> 00:41:09,833
Come on, you guys.

938
00:41:09,833 --> 00:41:11,100
You're not even trying.

939
00:41:11,100 --> 00:41:12,633
It's like your
trying to sabotage

940
00:41:12,633 --> 00:41:15,667
my William Sylvester
version of Trivial Pursuit.

941
00:41:15,667 --> 00:41:16,933
- We are, Mike.

942
00:41:16,933 --> 00:41:18,533
- We patently don't care
about William Sylvester.

943
00:41:18,533 --> 00:41:19,367
- Not a bit.

944
00:41:20,767 --> 00:41:21,867
- So you're conceding.

945
00:41:23,033 --> 00:41:24,500
- Yes, we concede.

946
00:41:24,500 --> 00:41:26,833
- Yes! Yes! Yes! Yes!

947
00:41:26,833 --> 00:41:28,300
I know more about William
Sylvester than you do.

948
00:41:28,300 --> 00:41:29,133
Or you.

949
00:41:29,133 --> 00:41:30,100
Yes, yes!

950
00:41:30,100 --> 00:41:31,200
(alarm blaring)
- Sad, really.

951
00:41:31,200 --> 00:41:33,633
- Woo hoo hoo, oh movie time!

952
00:41:35,733 --> 00:41:39,567
(doors creaking and thudding)

953
00:41:46,800 --> 00:41:47,667
- [Mike] Yes, yes.

954
00:41:47,667 --> 00:41:49,233
- [Tom] Your own game.

955
00:41:49,233 --> 00:41:51,033
- [Mike] Whew.

956
00:41:51,033 --> 00:41:54,100
- [Tom] And Buddy
Hackett on trumpet.

957
00:41:55,633 --> 00:41:57,033
- Down.

958
00:41:57,033 --> 00:41:58,267
Down.

959
00:41:58,267 --> 00:42:00,267
Let her clear.

960
00:42:00,267 --> 00:42:01,200
Keep her clear.

961
00:42:01,200 --> 00:42:02,433
- [Crow] Men not without hats.

962
00:42:02,433 --> 00:42:05,067
- [Man] All right,
lower it a bit.

963
00:42:07,567 --> 00:42:09,600
- Unique in
evolutionary biology,

964
00:42:09,600 --> 00:42:11,833
and you turn it
into a circus freak.

965
00:42:11,833 --> 00:42:13,033
Outrageous.

966
00:42:13,033 --> 00:42:14,767
Quite apart from the
fact that you stole it.

967
00:42:14,767 --> 00:42:15,967
- [Mike] Dorkin.

968
00:42:15,967 --> 00:42:16,767
- Now that's a
matter of opinion.

969
00:42:16,767 --> 00:42:18,200
- What's this all about?

970
00:42:18,200 --> 00:42:20,233
- We know absolutely
nothing about this animal.

971
00:42:20,233 --> 00:42:21,667
It's extremely dangerous.

972
00:42:21,667 --> 00:42:23,167
- We've managed to handle
it all right so far.

973
00:42:23,167 --> 00:42:24,667
- [Tom] One guy died, but.

974
00:42:24,667 --> 00:42:27,067
- They carry disease-bearing
parasites or unknown bacteria.

975
00:42:27,067 --> 00:42:28,567
- [Crow] Like me.

976
00:42:28,567 --> 00:42:30,033
- And you bring it into
the heart of a great city.

977
00:42:30,033 --> 00:42:31,533
- Look, what do you want?

978
00:42:31,533 --> 00:42:34,400
- First the opportunity
to make a complete study.

979
00:42:34,400 --> 00:42:35,500
- Okay.

980
00:42:35,500 --> 00:42:37,233
If it doesn't interfere
with business.

981
00:42:37,233 --> 00:42:38,967
- Believe me, gentlemen, once
the animal is been stored

982
00:42:38,967 --> 00:42:41,800
in Battersea Park, you'll
be given every facility.

983
00:42:41,800 --> 00:42:43,600
- I presume you've thought of
the need of giving the animal

984
00:42:43,600 --> 00:42:46,300
a tranquilizing drug
before you transport it?

985
00:42:46,300 --> 00:42:48,167
'Cause if you
haven't, you'd better.

986
00:42:48,167 --> 00:42:49,400
- Well, they bet on it.

987
00:42:50,700 --> 00:42:52,167
- Good day.

988
00:42:52,167 --> 00:42:53,467
- [Tom] Which one of
these guys is Dorkin?

989
00:42:53,467 --> 00:42:54,933
It's hard to tell
from this picture.

990
00:42:58,533 --> 00:43:01,100
- [Mike] Yes, Dorkin
means great entertainment.

991
00:43:01,100 --> 00:43:02,567
- [Crow] Dorkin
under the big top.

992
00:43:02,567 --> 00:43:04,400
Come see the delightful
Dorkin midgets

993
00:43:04,400 --> 00:43:06,367
and the funny Dorkin clowns.

994
00:43:07,800 --> 00:43:09,600
- [reporter] In our most
unusual telecast to date,

995
00:43:09,600 --> 00:43:12,367
we bring you the
arrival of Gorgo.

996
00:43:12,367 --> 00:43:14,533
This creature, which
should of been extinct

997
00:43:14,533 --> 00:43:18,133
10 million years ago, is
truly an awesome sight

998
00:43:18,133 --> 00:43:21,000
as it is transported though
the streets of modern London

999
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:23,267
on it's way to Battersea Park.

1000
00:43:23,267 --> 00:43:26,000
Of course, Londoners are
notoriously skeptical.

1001
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:28,233
And a good many
we've spoken too...

1002
00:43:28,233 --> 00:43:31,633
(viewers muttering loudly)

1003
00:43:31,633 --> 00:43:33,267
But this animal is real.

1004
00:43:33,267 --> 00:43:34,633
Take my word for it.

1005
00:43:34,633 --> 00:43:35,767
- [Mike] I'm Dennis Thatcher.

1006
00:43:35,767 --> 00:43:36,967
- [Reporter] The
police have cleared all

1007
00:43:36,967 --> 00:43:38,667
unauthorized traffic
along the route,

1008
00:43:38,667 --> 00:43:40,000
as a precautionary measure.

1009
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:42,400
Although we're assured
there's no danger,

1010
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:46,033
the creature has been given
massive doses of tranquilizer.

1011
00:43:46,033 --> 00:43:48,033
Much closer to this
thing, and I could do

1012
00:43:48,033 --> 00:43:50,300
with a bit of
tranquilizer myself.

1013
00:43:50,300 --> 00:43:51,700
Actually, it's no joke.

1014
00:43:51,700 --> 00:43:52,967
- [Crow] I need drugs.

1015
00:43:52,967 --> 00:43:54,200
- [Reporter] There are
rumors that the animal

1016
00:43:54,200 --> 00:43:56,233
has killed a number
of persons already

1017
00:43:56,233 --> 00:43:57,900
in the process of its capture.

1018
00:43:57,900 --> 00:44:00,233
- [Tom] But they were
all Irish, so it's okay.

1019
00:44:01,400 --> 00:44:02,633
- [Reporter] Now we can
see the circus crowds

1020
00:44:02,633 --> 00:44:04,433
at Battersea Park in
the festival gardens,

1021
00:44:04,433 --> 00:44:08,100
where Gorgo, as he is
called, we don't know why,

1022
00:44:08,100 --> 00:44:09,967
will be exhibited to the public.

1023
00:44:09,967 --> 00:44:11,233
- [Mike] And Hitler pulls up.

1024
00:44:11,233 --> 00:44:13,100
- Here we can see the
intrepid Captain Ryan,

1025
00:44:13,100 --> 00:44:15,267
the one with the cap,
getting out of the car

1026
00:44:15,267 --> 00:44:16,933
with his partner, Mr. Slade.

1027
00:44:16,933 --> 00:44:18,200
- [Crow] Flintstone!

1028
00:44:18,200 --> 00:44:19,767
- Here's the man who
captured the animal.

1029
00:44:19,767 --> 00:44:22,400
And I don't envy them the job.

1030
00:44:22,400 --> 00:44:24,967
And our own Mr. Dorkin,
of Dorkin's Circus,

1031
00:44:24,967 --> 00:44:26,100
in the checkered suit.

1032
00:44:26,100 --> 00:44:27,033
- [Crow] In the chicken suit?

1033
00:44:27,033 --> 00:44:27,967
- [Reporter] And by the way-

1034
00:44:27,967 --> 00:44:29,367
- [Mike] That I don't see.

1035
00:44:29,367 --> 00:44:32,200
- [Voiceover] Shortly at
Battersea Park, so stay with us.

1036
00:44:32,200 --> 00:44:34,900
And of course, ladies and
gentlemen, I realized at once

1037
00:44:34,900 --> 00:44:37,267
that this was the most
shattering scientific discovery

1038
00:44:37,267 --> 00:44:39,000
of the 20th century,
and so I contracted

1039
00:44:39,000 --> 00:44:40,400
for the exhibition
of the animal.

1040
00:44:40,400 --> 00:44:42,833
We've built a special enclosure,
you'll see it later on.

1041
00:44:42,833 --> 00:44:45,567
We rushed the job through,
just so everyone in London

1042
00:44:45,567 --> 00:44:48,167
would have an opportunity of
seeing Gorgo for themselves.

1043
00:44:48,167 --> 00:44:50,500
- I have a report from Dublin
that the Irish government

1044
00:44:50,500 --> 00:44:53,100
are going to take
legal proceedings to
recover the animal.

1045
00:44:53,100 --> 00:44:54,700
- Yes, I believe
that to be true.

1046
00:44:54,700 --> 00:44:55,700
Of course, the matter
will have to go through

1047
00:44:55,700 --> 00:44:57,233
the courts in the usual way.

1048
00:44:57,233 --> 00:44:58,767
And since I have no way
of knowing how quickly

1049
00:44:58,767 --> 00:45:00,233
they'll reach their
decision, I suggest

1050
00:45:00,233 --> 00:45:01,200
that anyone who
wants to see Gorgo

1051
00:45:01,200 --> 00:45:03,100
does so soon at Battersea Park.

1052
00:45:03,100 --> 00:45:04,267
- That name, Gorgo.

1053
00:45:04,267 --> 00:45:06,367
Does it have any
special significance?

1054
00:45:06,367 --> 00:45:08,400
- Yes sir, of course, the
monster of Greek mythology.

1055
00:45:08,400 --> 00:45:09,900
The Gorgons.

1056
00:45:09,900 --> 00:45:11,400
A creature so terrifying
that the mere sight of it

1057
00:45:11,400 --> 00:45:12,967
would turn a man to stone.

1058
00:45:12,967 --> 00:45:14,467
- [Mike] This is for Dorkin.

1059
00:45:14,467 --> 00:45:16,633
- How much money do you
expect to make out of it?

1060
00:45:16,633 --> 00:45:17,900
- I'll answer that
question later on.

1061
00:45:17,900 --> 00:45:19,700
- [Crow] Don't bother
the man, he's Dorkin.

1062
00:45:21,867 --> 00:45:22,800
- [Tom] Oh, they
have to clean up

1063
00:45:22,800 --> 00:45:24,267
after Mighty Joe Young first.

1064
00:45:26,200 --> 00:45:27,033
- Lower away.

1065
00:45:28,767 --> 00:45:29,600
- Lower away!

1066
00:45:30,733 --> 00:45:31,567
Easy!

1067
00:45:32,467 --> 00:45:33,633
- Okay, Tom.

1068
00:45:33,633 --> 00:45:34,567
- Easy does it!

1069
00:45:34,567 --> 00:45:35,667
- [Crow] One day at a time.

1070
00:45:35,667 --> 00:45:36,600
Be in the moment.

1071
00:45:36,600 --> 00:45:38,133
Don't sweat the small stuff.

1072
00:45:41,467 --> 00:45:42,967
- [Tom] The net's
digging into my groin,

1073
00:45:42,967 --> 00:45:44,000
if you could just...

1074
00:45:45,133 --> 00:45:46,200
- [Man] Fore!

1075
00:45:46,200 --> 00:45:47,367
- [Mike] Playing through.

1076
00:45:47,367 --> 00:45:48,467
- [Man] Fore!

1077
00:45:49,567 --> 00:45:51,300
- Tranquilizer
seems to be working.

1078
00:45:51,300 --> 00:45:52,333
- [Crow] I'm very mellow.

1079
00:45:52,333 --> 00:45:53,167
- So far.

1080
00:45:55,900 --> 00:45:58,500
- [Tom] Is there a union
for monster wranglers here?

1081
00:46:01,100 --> 00:46:03,067
- [Mike] Wanna help, Joe?

1082
00:46:03,067 --> 00:46:05,767
- [Crow] They're trying to
exhume William Howard Taft.

1083
00:46:11,167 --> 00:46:12,033
- [Man] Wire cutters.

1084
00:46:12,033 --> 00:46:13,533
- Wire cutters, quick.

1085
00:46:13,533 --> 00:46:14,367
- Okay.

1086
00:46:17,000 --> 00:46:18,067
- Cut the net, boys.

1087
00:46:19,467 --> 00:46:21,967
- [Tom] He has a splinter,
let's take care of that first.

1088
00:46:25,800 --> 00:46:27,733
- [Mike] Mm, world's
biggest porchetta.

1089
00:46:32,567 --> 00:46:34,067
- [Crow] Look we all know
where this is heading.

1090
00:46:34,067 --> 00:46:36,667
Can we just start
tossing Brits around?

1091
00:46:38,833 --> 00:46:40,667
- [Tom] Mr. Gorgo, is it
true you had an affair

1092
00:46:40,667 --> 00:46:41,567
with Judith Asner?

1093
00:46:43,733 --> 00:46:45,733
- That's a hell of
a dumb thing to do.

1094
00:46:49,300 --> 00:46:50,433
- [Tom] I'm up, I'm up.

1095
00:46:54,033 --> 00:46:56,733
- [Crow] Yeah,
I've got net hair.

1096
00:46:56,733 --> 00:46:58,167
(Gorgo roaring)

1097
00:46:58,167 --> 00:46:59,433
♪ I crush you ♪

1098
00:46:59,433 --> 00:47:02,267
♪ You get crushed ♪

1099
00:47:03,700 --> 00:47:06,833
- [Mike] This is like
England's 178th finest hour.

1100
00:47:08,033 --> 00:47:08,867
- [Tom] Help us, help!

1101
00:47:11,700 --> 00:47:13,533
- [Mike] The Japanese that
lies dormant in all of us

1102
00:47:13,533 --> 00:47:15,700
comes to the surface in
situations like these.

1103
00:47:15,700 --> 00:47:18,300
- [Tom] Mm-hmm.

1104
00:47:18,300 --> 00:47:19,600
Oops.

1105
00:47:19,600 --> 00:47:20,700
- Over here!

1106
00:47:20,700 --> 00:47:22,000
- [Crow] Sit.

1107
00:47:22,000 --> 00:47:23,533
You sit.

1108
00:47:23,533 --> 00:47:25,300
- [Mike] Hey, best
teeth in England.

1109
00:47:28,033 --> 00:47:28,933
- [Tom] Free Huey.

1110
00:47:32,633 --> 00:47:35,733
(shouting indistinctly)

1111
00:47:35,733 --> 00:47:38,600
- [Crow] This is truly a black
day in the history of Dorkin.

1112
00:47:38,600 --> 00:47:40,633
- [Mike] Will this
stop England's Dorkin?

1113
00:47:44,267 --> 00:47:46,267
- [Tom] He looks like
a chocolate bunny.

1114
00:47:47,667 --> 00:47:48,867
- [Mike] Aw, looks like Crow.

1115
00:47:50,433 --> 00:47:53,200
- [Crow] Ooh, I didn't know
elephants exploded on impact.

1116
00:47:53,200 --> 00:47:54,200
- [Tom] Wow.

1117
00:47:56,267 --> 00:47:57,700
- Come on, this way.

1118
00:47:57,700 --> 00:48:00,100
- We'll take the other side.

1119
00:48:02,800 --> 00:48:04,200
- [Mike] We've
installed this system

1120
00:48:04,200 --> 00:48:06,033
rather than the traditional
hand towels, Gorgo.

1121
00:48:06,033 --> 00:48:08,267
It prevents chapping
and spread of disease.

1122
00:48:09,367 --> 00:48:11,067
(Gorgo roaring)

1123
00:48:11,067 --> 00:48:12,567
- [Tom] I left
instructions with Mothra.

1124
00:48:12,567 --> 00:48:14,733
If I don't come back, he's
gonna wreck this joint!

1125
00:48:16,600 --> 00:48:18,467
- Give him another blast!

1126
00:48:18,467 --> 00:48:19,800
- [Crow] Embarass him!

1127
00:48:19,800 --> 00:48:21,600
Ask him about the
size of his brain!

1128
00:48:28,000 --> 00:48:30,033
- [Mike] Look, can I
just eat Manchester?

1129
00:48:35,367 --> 00:48:36,400
- [Crow] Oh, right in the store.

1130
00:48:36,400 --> 00:48:37,200
- Wow.

1131
00:48:38,400 --> 00:48:40,400
- [Mike] Quit draggin'
tail, Gorgo, come on.

1132
00:48:47,700 --> 00:48:50,600
Poor guy was just there for
take your brother to work day.

1133
00:48:53,633 --> 00:48:55,800
- [Tom] Gorgo gave
himself a little time out.

1134
00:48:58,500 --> 00:49:01,100
- [Crow] Buses really run
frequently here in London.

1135
00:49:04,267 --> 00:49:06,000
- [Mike] Come see Gorgo,
he'll kill your family.

1136
00:49:06,000 --> 00:49:07,967
Come get killed by Gorgo.

1137
00:49:11,167 --> 00:49:13,033
- [Crow] Dorkin with
John Collins' big dipper.

1138
00:49:18,133 --> 00:49:20,833
Happy couples enjoying
Dorkin all night long.

1139
00:49:21,867 --> 00:49:24,600
(cheerful music)

1140
00:49:27,767 --> 00:49:29,200
- [Mike] Hey, it is balloons.

1141
00:49:33,800 --> 00:49:35,200
Insulation on a stick.

1142
00:49:36,667 --> 00:49:38,833
- [Reporter] Everybody in the
world is talking about Gorgo,

1143
00:49:38,833 --> 00:49:42,467
but only you can see him,
and only here at Dorkins.

1144
00:49:43,867 --> 00:49:45,567
Two lanes, both sides, five
shillings, ladies and gentlemen.

1145
00:49:45,567 --> 00:49:47,333
Bring the children,
very instructive.

1146
00:49:47,333 --> 00:49:48,500
This way.

1147
00:49:48,500 --> 00:49:50,500
Hurry, hurry,
hurry to see Gorgo.

1148
00:49:50,500 --> 00:49:53,167
The last remaining link
with prehistoric times.

1149
00:49:53,167 --> 00:49:55,000
Only five shillings,
ladies and gentlemen.

1150
00:49:55,000 --> 00:49:56,667
- [Crow] Hooray for Dorkin!

1151
00:49:57,800 --> 00:49:59,500
- There a still a
few remaining places.

1152
00:49:59,500 --> 00:50:00,733
Two lanes, both sides.

1153
00:50:00,733 --> 00:50:02,467
Bring the children,
very instructive.

1154
00:50:02,467 --> 00:50:04,467
What's that, madam,
looks like your husband?

1155
00:50:04,467 --> 00:50:06,400
Go inside to find out.

1156
00:50:06,400 --> 00:50:10,767
65-foot high Gorgo, and
heavier than six elephants.

1157
00:50:10,767 --> 00:50:12,500
- [Tom] Oh, that this
too too solid flesh

1158
00:50:12,500 --> 00:50:15,300
would melt, thaw, and
resolve itself into a dew.

1159
00:50:18,367 --> 00:50:20,333
- [Crow] It's reject
day at the circus.

1160
00:50:22,467 --> 00:50:25,067
- [Man] Ice cream here,
get your ice cream.

1161
00:50:25,067 --> 00:50:27,733
♪ Mr. Bojangles ♪

1162
00:50:28,967 --> 00:50:30,233
- Peanuts, popcorn!

1163
00:50:30,233 --> 00:50:31,800
- [Tom] Hey, John Quincy Adams.

1164
00:50:33,667 --> 00:50:35,433
- Get your peanuts here.

1165
00:50:35,433 --> 00:50:37,033
(Gorgo roaring)

1166
00:50:37,033 --> 00:50:37,933
♪ It's not unusual ♪

1167
00:50:37,933 --> 00:50:39,133
- [Crow] Thank you.

1168
00:50:39,133 --> 00:50:42,000
(crowd chattering)

1169
00:50:44,067 --> 00:50:46,200
- [Mike] Mm, brain matter.

1170
00:50:49,833 --> 00:50:51,533
- [Tom] Hi there,
cutie, heh heh.

1171
00:50:54,800 --> 00:50:56,333
- [Crow] Hey ladies,
I know Gorgo.

1172
00:50:56,333 --> 00:50:57,133
Wanna meet him?

1173
00:51:00,400 --> 00:51:02,400
- Looks like a full house.

1174
00:51:02,400 --> 00:51:03,333
- Not bad.

1175
00:51:03,333 --> 00:51:04,600
- [Mike] That's why I'm Dorkin.

1176
00:51:07,767 --> 00:51:11,100
♪ Don't break my heart,
my achy breaky heart ♪

1177
00:51:11,100 --> 00:51:13,900
- [Crow] Great, Colonel Tom
Parker's gonna manage Gorgo.

1178
00:51:19,000 --> 00:51:21,267
- [Tom] Next show in
half an hour, Mr. Gorgo.

1179
00:51:22,267 --> 00:51:23,100
- Hi, Sam.

1180
00:51:25,167 --> 00:51:26,367
I was driving into town.

1181
00:51:27,967 --> 00:51:30,633
Thought you might like to come
along, have a couple drinks.

1182
00:51:30,633 --> 00:51:32,100
- I'm having a couple of drinks.

1183
00:51:32,100 --> 00:51:34,533
- [Tom] I'm just tearing
through the pink squirrels.

1184
00:51:34,533 --> 00:51:35,700
- How's business?

1185
00:51:35,700 --> 00:51:37,467
- [Sam] Great, great.

1186
00:51:37,467 --> 00:51:38,700
- Why don't you get
out of this dump?

1187
00:51:38,700 --> 00:51:39,500
Live a little.

1188
00:51:40,933 --> 00:51:42,967
I got a suite of rooms at
the best hotel in town.

1189
00:51:42,967 --> 00:51:45,233
- [Crow] Gorgo's got
the big bed, but.

1190
00:51:45,233 --> 00:51:46,133
- This suits us.

1191
00:51:47,533 --> 00:51:49,133
(elephant screeching)

1192
00:51:49,133 --> 00:51:51,267
- [Mike] Great, Gorgo
ate another elephant.

1193
00:51:54,867 --> 00:51:57,400
- Look Joe, these
animals are gone crazy.

1194
00:51:57,400 --> 00:51:59,433
Something's gonna
happen, I can feel it.

1195
00:51:59,433 --> 00:52:00,533
Mike's already been killed.

1196
00:52:00,533 --> 00:52:01,733
Who's gonna be next?

1197
00:52:01,733 --> 00:52:02,667
- I'm as sorry about
Mike as you are.

1198
00:52:02,667 --> 00:52:03,967
It's a tough break, that's all.

1199
00:52:03,967 --> 00:52:06,467
- Well, he's got a
wife and two kids.

1200
00:52:06,467 --> 00:52:08,933
- Okay, well we'll
send her some money.

1201
00:52:08,933 --> 00:52:10,133
- But he's dead.

1202
00:52:10,133 --> 00:52:11,367
- [Tom] Okay, we'll send
her another husband.

1203
00:52:11,367 --> 00:52:12,933
- There's gonna be a
lot more tough breaks

1204
00:52:12,933 --> 00:52:14,733
before this is over.

1205
00:52:14,733 --> 00:52:17,233
- You've been listening to
your new partner too much.

1206
00:52:18,733 --> 00:52:20,200
- I've been listening to myself.

1207
00:52:20,200 --> 00:52:21,333
(phone ringing)

1208
00:52:21,333 --> 00:52:22,700
- [Crow] Oh,
that'll be me again.

1209
00:52:25,400 --> 00:52:26,333
- Hello.

1210
00:52:26,333 --> 00:52:27,667
(Tom growls)

1211
00:52:27,667 --> 00:52:28,667
Yeah, just a moment.

1212
00:52:29,733 --> 00:52:31,000
It's the professor.

1213
00:52:31,000 --> 00:52:32,167
Says they've been
trying to find ya.

1214
00:52:32,167 --> 00:52:32,967
- [Tom] Do you
know where you are?

1215
00:52:32,967 --> 00:52:33,833
- This time of night?

1216
00:52:34,933 --> 00:52:36,533
Hello?

1217
00:52:36,533 --> 00:52:37,667
Yeah.

1218
00:52:37,667 --> 00:52:38,800
- [Crow] I'm
smoking, sure, yeah.

1219
00:52:38,800 --> 00:52:39,967
- Yeah if you say so.

1220
00:52:41,400 --> 00:52:42,233
Okay.

1221
00:52:43,400 --> 00:52:44,700
- [Mike] Well, I'm
suppose to hit you.

1222
00:52:44,700 --> 00:52:46,833
- Spotted something
out, you coming along?

1223
00:52:46,833 --> 00:52:48,400
- Yeah, sure.

1224
00:52:48,400 --> 00:52:50,567
- [Crow] Then we'll go
beat up some clowns.

1225
00:52:50,567 --> 00:52:53,300
(cheerful music)

1226
00:52:55,067 --> 00:52:57,400
- [Tom] But I don't want
to be Billy and mummy.

1227
00:53:03,100 --> 00:53:05,333
- [Mike] I hope Moneypenny's
happy to see me.

1228
00:53:06,567 --> 00:53:08,867
- [Crow] Holy worm's
busiest day ever.

1229
00:53:08,867 --> 00:53:09,867
- Come in gentlemen.

1230
00:53:11,567 --> 00:53:12,633
Captain Ryan.

1231
00:53:12,633 --> 00:53:14,133
- [Tom] Yes.

1232
00:53:14,133 --> 00:53:16,733
- The creature you captured
is not an adult specimen.

1233
00:53:16,733 --> 00:53:18,100
- You mean it isn't fully grown?

1234
00:53:18,100 --> 00:53:19,600
- No.

1235
00:53:19,600 --> 00:53:21,400
In fact, we believe it is
in rather early infancy.

1236
00:53:21,400 --> 00:53:23,733
- [Tom] You'll
need to change him.

1237
00:53:23,733 --> 00:53:25,167
- You trying to say there
may be a fully grown

1238
00:53:25,167 --> 00:53:26,833
one of these things
around somewhere?

1239
00:53:26,833 --> 00:53:28,467
- It's a fair assumption.

1240
00:53:28,467 --> 00:53:31,400
Where there are offspring,
there are generally parents.

1241
00:53:31,400 --> 00:53:33,267
- How big would a
full-grown one be?

1242
00:53:33,267 --> 00:53:34,767
- Approximate guess.

1243
00:53:34,767 --> 00:53:36,900
- [Mike] According to my
unnecessarily large book.

1244
00:53:38,233 --> 00:53:39,267
- [Professor] The infant.

1245
00:53:39,267 --> 00:53:40,600
- [Crow] The out-fant.

1246
00:53:40,600 --> 00:53:41,633
- [Professor] The adult.

1247
00:53:43,633 --> 00:53:45,233
- That would make him
nearly 200 feet tall.

1248
00:53:45,233 --> 00:53:46,867
- At the very least.

1249
00:53:46,867 --> 00:53:48,700
- Obviously the proper
authorities must be
notified at once.

1250
00:53:48,700 --> 00:53:50,067
- What are you trying to do?

1251
00:53:50,067 --> 00:53:51,533
Stir up a whole hornet's nest?

1252
00:53:51,533 --> 00:53:54,267
Because of a few calculations
made on a piece of paper?

1253
00:53:54,267 --> 00:53:55,467
Nuts to that.

1254
00:53:55,467 --> 00:53:56,600
- I'm afraid, Captain
Ryan, the decision

1255
00:53:56,600 --> 00:53:58,267
is no longer in your hands.

1256
00:54:00,600 --> 00:54:02,100
- [Tom] One, two, three, step.

1257
00:54:02,100 --> 00:54:03,667
Two, no wrong.

1258
00:54:03,667 --> 00:54:05,367
Practice, practice, practice.

1259
00:54:05,367 --> 00:54:06,200
Two, three.

1260
00:54:07,633 --> 00:54:09,967
- [Mike] Boy, these late
shows, nobody ever comes.

1261
00:54:11,700 --> 00:54:14,000
- [Crow] Hey kid,
wanna play me eat you?

1262
00:54:19,267 --> 00:54:20,767
- [Tom] What's that?

1263
00:54:20,767 --> 00:54:23,900
Oh, pain! (shouting)

1264
00:54:23,900 --> 00:54:24,833
What did I just experience?

1265
00:54:24,833 --> 00:54:27,567
Oh yeah, pain! (shouting)

1266
00:54:29,933 --> 00:54:32,633
(dog howling)

1267
00:54:32,633 --> 00:54:35,233
- [Mike] Ooh, he's got
Betty Davis eye, real bad.

1268
00:54:35,233 --> 00:54:36,067
- [Crow] Yikes.

1269
00:54:37,500 --> 00:54:39,633
- [Tom] I held my breath for
a million years, did you see?

1270
00:54:42,467 --> 00:54:43,967
- [Mike] Quiet, I'm
finishing a monologue

1271
00:54:43,967 --> 00:54:45,133
for Billy Whitelaw.

1272
00:54:48,233 --> 00:54:51,167
- [Crow] Ow, I stubbed
my toe on a duplex.

1273
00:54:53,633 --> 00:54:55,600
- [Tom] It's the road
company of Stomp.

1274
00:54:59,900 --> 00:55:01,633
- [Mike] I'm the
new harbor master.

1275
00:55:02,833 --> 00:55:05,833
(suspenseful music)

1276
00:55:10,500 --> 00:55:12,200
- [Tom] Ah, this feels great.

1277
00:55:12,200 --> 00:55:13,500
Oh, my achin' dogs.

1278
00:55:17,900 --> 00:55:21,767
- Admiral Brooks, I realize
the situation is fantastic.

1279
00:55:21,767 --> 00:55:24,200
- One expects the fantastic
from your scientists these days.

1280
00:55:24,200 --> 00:55:25,467
- [Mike] Stupid squid.

1281
00:55:25,467 --> 00:55:27,633
- Tell me Admiral,
have you ever seen

1282
00:55:27,633 --> 00:55:29,233
the animal that was captured?

1283
00:55:29,233 --> 00:55:30,700
- I'm afraid I
haven't had the time.

1284
00:55:30,700 --> 00:55:32,900
- Then perhaps you
should take the time.

1285
00:55:32,900 --> 00:55:36,867
And when you do, remember it
first appeared at Nada Island.

1286
00:55:38,267 --> 00:55:39,967
- Monitor reports there's
been no communication

1287
00:55:39,967 --> 00:55:41,700
with Nada Island
for three days, sir.

1288
00:55:41,700 --> 00:55:42,533
- [Tom] Thank you, Timmy.

1289
00:55:42,533 --> 00:55:43,367
- Three days?

1290
00:55:44,700 --> 00:55:48,600
- [Crow] Oh, and Pinafore's
about to start, sir.

1291
00:55:48,600 --> 00:55:51,000
- Her majesty's air craft
carrier, Royal Open.

1292
00:55:51,000 --> 00:55:53,500
From the Admiral T. Whitehall.

1293
00:55:53,500 --> 00:55:57,567
Proceed to area N34, and
report any unusual sightings

1294
00:55:57,567 --> 00:56:00,900
or conditions in
vicinity of Nada Island.

1295
00:56:00,900 --> 00:56:01,967
This is priority A.

1296
00:56:03,000 --> 00:56:03,967
Repeat.

1297
00:56:03,967 --> 00:56:05,533
This is priority A.

1298
00:56:07,967 --> 00:56:09,800
♪ One ♪

1299
00:56:13,800 --> 00:56:15,333
- [Crow] Meanwhile,
bits of the empire

1300
00:56:15,333 --> 00:56:18,400
are falling away, so long,
India, goodbye, Rhodesia.

1301
00:56:22,500 --> 00:56:24,067
- [Mike] Talmud,
Talmud, oh wait.

1302
00:56:24,067 --> 00:56:26,800
I mean, Torah, Torah.

1303
00:56:26,800 --> 00:56:30,067
(Tom murmuring)

1304
00:56:30,067 --> 00:56:32,167
- [Crow] Leave me
alone, get away from me.

1305
00:56:35,700 --> 00:56:38,233
- [Mike] The SS
Curly has arrived.

1306
00:56:38,233 --> 00:56:41,733
(imitating Three Stooges)

1307
00:56:42,900 --> 00:56:44,933
- [Crow] Hmm, there's
a spot on my window.

1308
00:56:45,933 --> 00:56:47,300
- [Mike] I'm drawing Binky.

1309
00:56:48,400 --> 00:56:50,300
- Reconnaissance to corvette.

1310
00:56:50,300 --> 00:56:52,500
Alter course to three-one-zero.

1311
00:56:52,500 --> 00:56:54,833
Repeat, course three-one-zero.

1312
00:56:56,133 --> 00:56:57,600
(alarm blaring)

1313
00:56:57,600 --> 00:57:00,067
- [Tomorrow] Soytenly. Nyuk,
nyuk, nyuk, nyuk, nyuk.

1314
00:57:00,067 --> 00:57:00,867
- Right.

1315
00:57:01,733 --> 00:57:02,733
They spotted something, sir.

1316
00:57:02,733 --> 00:57:04,433
- Port four points.

1317
00:57:04,433 --> 00:57:05,700
- Port, four points.

1318
00:57:05,700 --> 00:57:07,133
- Aye-aye.

1319
00:57:07,133 --> 00:57:08,400
- [Crow] Going
over our own wake.

1320
00:57:08,400 --> 00:57:09,333
It's fun.

1321
00:57:10,500 --> 00:57:11,833
- [Mike] Deploy
pitching machine.

1322
00:57:15,100 --> 00:57:16,400
- [Tom] The order comes in.

1323
00:57:16,400 --> 00:57:17,233
Kill Willy.

1324
00:57:23,067 --> 00:57:25,033
- [Crow] She has perm
rods in her hair.

1325
00:57:26,800 --> 00:57:28,267
- [Mike] Man, I love krill.

1326
00:57:28,267 --> 00:57:29,467
- Fire number one turret.

1327
00:57:29,467 --> 00:57:31,800
(turrets booming)

1328
00:57:31,800 --> 00:57:34,700
- [Tom] Wait wait, we can't
all be number one turret.

1329
00:57:38,533 --> 00:57:39,700
- [Crow] Whoa.

1330
00:57:41,667 --> 00:57:43,433
- [Mike] Look at this,
doormen just roam

1331
00:57:43,433 --> 00:57:46,100
the streets of London
looking for people to serve.

1332
00:57:46,100 --> 00:57:47,667
(telegraphs beeping)

1333
00:57:47,667 --> 00:57:48,933
- [Tom] I will not
fake distress calls.

1334
00:57:48,933 --> 00:57:50,500
I will not fake distress calls.

1335
00:57:53,233 --> 00:57:54,433
- Admiral Brooks.

1336
00:57:54,433 --> 00:57:56,767
- [Crow] We decided
England is shaped funny.

1337
00:57:56,767 --> 00:57:58,767
- Gentlemen, you've
been asked to come here

1338
00:57:58,767 --> 00:58:00,633
to supply us with any
information in your position

1339
00:58:00,633 --> 00:58:01,767
which might be helpful.

1340
00:58:01,767 --> 00:58:04,200
Nada Island has been destroyed.

1341
00:58:04,200 --> 00:58:07,267
Reconnaissance aircraft
has sighted the creature

1342
00:58:07,267 --> 00:58:08,500
in this area here.

1343
00:58:08,500 --> 00:58:09,867
- If you ask me, sir,
that looks as though

1344
00:58:09,867 --> 00:58:10,733
the thing's heading for England.

1345
00:58:10,733 --> 00:58:12,167
- Quite.

1346
00:58:12,167 --> 00:58:14,000
Her majesty herself
called for an explanation.

1347
00:58:14,000 --> 00:58:15,367
Do you have one?

1348
00:58:15,367 --> 00:58:17,000
- Well, we were draining
a stream of water on it

1349
00:58:17,000 --> 00:58:18,433
during the passage.

1350
00:58:18,433 --> 00:58:20,833
It was Professor
Flahherty's instructions.

1351
00:58:20,833 --> 00:58:23,133
Maybe he left some
sort of track.

1352
00:58:23,133 --> 00:58:23,967
- I see.

1353
00:58:23,967 --> 00:58:24,800
- [Mike] Way to go.

1354
00:58:24,800 --> 00:58:25,967
- Is that possible?

1355
00:58:25,967 --> 00:58:27,700
- Yes, it's possible.

1356
00:58:27,700 --> 00:58:30,267
- The Royal Oak for
Admiral Brooks, please.

1357
00:58:30,267 --> 00:58:32,533
Royal Oak coming in
for Admiral Brooks.

1358
00:58:32,533 --> 00:58:33,467
- Good.

1359
00:58:33,467 --> 00:58:34,633
- [Crow] My charcoal is here.

1360
00:58:34,633 --> 00:58:37,300
- They report at
least two direct hits.

1361
00:58:37,300 --> 00:58:38,533
- You killed it?

1362
00:58:38,533 --> 00:58:40,333
- It's disappeared
beneath the sea.

1363
00:58:40,333 --> 00:58:42,000
They saturated the area
with death charges.

1364
00:58:42,000 --> 00:58:43,700
No living thing could escape.

1365
00:58:43,700 --> 00:58:45,033
It's certainly been killed.

1366
00:58:47,500 --> 00:58:48,800
- [Tom] We killed it, Moe.

1367
00:58:50,867 --> 00:58:51,800
(dramatic music)

1368
00:58:51,800 --> 00:58:53,133
- [Mike] A giant sea camel.

1369
00:58:57,233 --> 00:58:58,933
- [Tom] Ah, pork-fed grits.

1370
00:59:08,767 --> 00:59:11,167
- [Mike] There's got to
be a morning after, sir.

1371
00:59:13,300 --> 00:59:14,533
- Capsized?

1372
00:59:14,533 --> 00:59:16,500
- [Crow] I've got to
have my cap sized.

1373
00:59:18,100 --> 00:59:19,433
- With every man aboard.

1374
00:59:21,633 --> 00:59:23,267
- This is terrible.

1375
00:59:23,267 --> 00:59:24,700
- [Mike] I see it
as an opportunity.

1376
00:59:24,700 --> 00:59:26,767
- Well, all right, now what?

1377
00:59:26,767 --> 00:59:28,467
- What will you do?

1378
00:59:28,467 --> 00:59:30,500
- Turn that thing at
the festival gardens
loose, what else?

1379
00:59:30,500 --> 00:59:32,667
Take it back to the sea,
while we still got the chance.

1380
00:59:32,667 --> 00:59:33,800
- What the hell's
a matter with you?

1381
00:59:33,800 --> 00:59:35,233
This is the 20th century.

1382
00:59:35,233 --> 00:59:36,733
There must be someway of
handling an over-grown animal.

1383
00:59:36,733 --> 00:59:39,067
- There's no doubt that
we can stop the creature.

1384
00:59:39,067 --> 00:59:40,300
- [Tom] Yes.

1385
00:59:40,300 --> 00:59:42,033
- We'll call you if
we need you again.

1386
00:59:42,033 --> 00:59:43,267
Good evening gentlemen.

1387
00:59:43,267 --> 00:59:44,633
- [Crow] Ah but could
you postpone your plans

1388
00:59:44,633 --> 00:59:46,700
to bring the
500-foot stick insect

1389
00:59:46,700 --> 00:59:48,000
into the middle of London?

1390
00:59:49,767 --> 00:59:52,367
(upbeat music)

1391
00:59:57,133 --> 01:00:00,167
- [Tom] Dorkin just
doesn't seem that same now.

1392
01:00:00,167 --> 01:00:04,300
- [Mike] Nope.

1393
01:00:04,300 --> 01:00:06,300
Good night Gorgo.
- Roar.

1394
01:00:07,967 --> 01:00:09,300
- [Tom] The clowns let
me borrow their car.

1395
01:00:09,300 --> 01:00:10,133
It's great!

1396
01:00:13,000 --> 01:00:14,567
- [Mike] Well, I do
live in a trailer.

1397
01:00:14,567 --> 01:00:17,000
Better listen to some Bob
Seger and watch Nascar.

1398
01:00:17,000 --> 01:00:19,600
- Sam, stop, he'll kill you.

1399
01:00:19,600 --> 01:00:20,933
- I'm turning him loose.

1400
01:00:20,933 --> 01:00:22,133
- Stop it, Sam!

1401
01:00:22,133 --> 01:00:24,567
He'll kill you!
- Only chance.

1402
01:00:25,600 --> 01:00:26,533
Let him go!

1403
01:00:29,067 --> 01:00:31,833
- [Crow] Don't
vomit on me again.

1404
01:00:37,400 --> 01:00:40,033
(Gorgo roaring)

1405
01:00:40,033 --> 01:00:41,900
- [Tom] Don't fuss
so over me, please.

1406
01:00:43,100 --> 01:00:44,633
- [Mike] Fem-punch.

1407
01:00:44,633 --> 01:00:47,933
(Gorgo roaring)

1408
01:00:47,933 --> 01:00:49,767
- Stop it Sam, you're crazy.

1409
01:00:49,767 --> 01:00:51,433
- [Crow] Hmm, I
like punching you.

1410
01:00:53,300 --> 01:00:54,700
- [Tom] We're going
to the vet, right?

1411
01:00:54,700 --> 01:00:55,533
- Sam!

1412
01:00:55,533 --> 01:00:56,767
Are you all right? Sam.

1413
01:00:56,767 --> 01:00:58,367
Are you all right?

1414
01:00:58,367 --> 01:00:59,600
Sam.

1415
01:00:59,600 --> 01:01:00,533
- [Tom] You made
the pants too long.

1416
01:01:00,533 --> 01:01:01,433
- Sam.
- Go on, kid.

1417
01:01:03,767 --> 01:01:05,200
♪ Fail, Britainia ♪

1418
01:01:05,200 --> 01:01:08,867
♪ You never ruled the waves ♪

1419
01:01:10,300 --> 01:01:13,067
(turret booming)

1420
01:01:14,800 --> 01:01:15,633
- [Mike] Ah, here.

1421
01:01:19,000 --> 01:01:20,700
- [Crow] Well, we've
taken out the QE2,

1422
01:01:20,700 --> 01:01:23,400
the Bismark, and the Loveboat,
but we've missed Gorgo.

1423
01:01:24,867 --> 01:01:26,000
- [Tom] Oh, look what
the neighbors are doing.

1424
01:01:26,000 --> 01:01:27,567
- Reconnaissance to corvette.

1425
01:01:27,567 --> 01:01:28,800
Observation negative.

1426
01:01:28,800 --> 01:01:30,033
Repeat, negative.

1427
01:01:31,500 --> 01:01:32,767
- [Mike] Can I get
some help here?

1428
01:01:32,767 --> 01:01:34,100
There's usually a
guy with a thing.

1429
01:01:41,100 --> 01:01:43,700
- Fire with all hands,
the admiralty has deployed

1430
01:01:43,700 --> 01:01:47,167
major forces of the NATO fleet
off the mouth of the estuary

1431
01:01:47,167 --> 01:01:49,967
in an attempt to locate
and destroy the monster

1432
01:01:49,967 --> 01:01:52,133
even before it reaches
the coastal defenses.

1433
01:01:53,700 --> 01:01:55,467
- [Crow] They're
firing geese at him.

1434
01:01:55,467 --> 01:01:58,300
(dramatic music)

1435
01:01:58,300 --> 01:01:59,967
- [Mike] L, L, L.

1436
01:01:59,967 --> 01:02:00,767
L, L, L.

1437
01:02:03,100 --> 01:02:06,000
- [Crow] R R R, R R R R.

1438
01:02:08,733 --> 01:02:10,100
- [Tom] They must
get everything.

1439
01:02:11,800 --> 01:02:14,033
- Still tracking west 25.

1440
01:02:14,033 --> 01:02:14,933
- Roger task force.

1441
01:02:14,933 --> 01:02:16,100
Search 20 degrees west.

1442
01:02:17,933 --> 01:02:18,933
- [Mike] Ma!

1443
01:02:30,933 --> 01:02:32,933
- [Crow] Oh, he's so cute.

1444
01:02:32,933 --> 01:02:34,867
- [Tom] Is Roger looking at me?

1445
01:02:34,867 --> 01:02:37,833
- [Mike] Look at those
two, it's disgusting.

1446
01:02:37,833 --> 01:02:40,567
(dramatic music)

1447
01:02:47,000 --> 01:02:48,233
- [Crow] You know at this point,

1448
01:02:48,233 --> 01:02:50,633
I'd be glad to see Cher
straddling these guns.

1449
01:02:52,833 --> 01:02:54,100
- [Tom] Isn't this cutting into

1450
01:02:54,100 --> 01:02:55,567
the navy's groping
and harassment time?

1451
01:02:57,800 --> 01:03:00,633
- [Mike] The English reject
beer that's too cold.

1452
01:03:00,633 --> 01:03:03,700
(explosions booming)

1453
01:03:07,267 --> 01:03:08,767
- [Crow] This is the
equivalent of the Navy

1454
01:03:08,767 --> 01:03:11,133
getting drunk and throwing
cherry bombs over board.

1455
01:03:17,000 --> 01:03:19,433
- [Tom] 10 whales are dead, sir.

1456
01:03:19,433 --> 01:03:22,167
(dramatic music)

1457
01:03:24,333 --> 01:03:26,900
- [Mike] We are a
submarine, right?

1458
01:03:26,900 --> 01:03:27,733
Good.

1459
01:03:29,633 --> 01:03:31,667
- [Crow] Ooh, ooh, I think
I just saw Larry Hovas.

1460
01:03:31,667 --> 01:03:32,600
- [Mike] Where?

1461
01:03:32,600 --> 01:03:34,833
- [Man] That's the cue, sir.

1462
01:03:36,200 --> 01:03:38,300
- [Tom] Oops, we left the
captain in the accounting tower.

1463
01:03:44,333 --> 01:03:46,467
- Steady as she goes
for torpedo check.

1464
01:03:46,467 --> 01:03:49,467
Course two-seven-five,
range 1500.

1465
01:03:49,467 --> 01:03:51,100
- [Man] Course
two-seven-five, sir.

1466
01:03:51,100 --> 01:03:52,833
Firing check.

1467
01:03:52,833 --> 01:03:54,467
- Ready to open fire, sir.

1468
01:03:55,400 --> 01:03:56,900
- Tile one.

1469
01:03:56,900 --> 01:03:57,600
- [Mike] Movie much longer
than it would've been

1470
01:03:57,600 --> 01:03:58,200
without us, sir.

1471
01:03:59,867 --> 01:04:01,033
- Fire two.

1472
01:04:01,033 --> 01:04:02,500
- [Crow] Can't talk now, bye.

1473
01:04:05,167 --> 01:04:06,733
- Attacking target
in search area.

1474
01:04:06,733 --> 01:04:09,400
- [Tom] I need a bigger monitor.

1475
01:04:10,967 --> 01:04:12,133
- Contact broken.

1476
01:04:12,133 --> 01:04:12,933
Target moving
toward the estuary.

1477
01:04:12,933 --> 01:04:14,600
Too fast to follow.

1478
01:04:14,600 --> 01:04:16,033
- Getting submarine control.

1479
01:04:16,033 --> 01:04:17,300
Sector two-eight.

1480
01:04:19,400 --> 01:04:21,300
- We're just closing the
last of the submarine nets

1481
01:04:21,300 --> 01:04:22,267
now, sir.

1482
01:04:22,267 --> 01:04:23,300
- Mm-hmm.
- Yes, sir.

1483
01:04:24,700 --> 01:04:27,700
- [Mike] This must keep
foul balls out of the ocean.

1484
01:04:31,200 --> 01:04:32,033
- [Tom] Cigarette alert.

1485
01:04:32,033 --> 01:04:32,900
Everybody smoke.

1486
01:04:32,900 --> 01:04:34,467
(alarm blaring)

1487
01:04:34,467 --> 01:04:35,300
- Yes?

1488
01:04:35,300 --> 01:04:36,833
Yes!

1489
01:04:36,833 --> 01:04:40,200
Oh no, the nets have been torn,
and the connection is gone.

1490
01:04:40,200 --> 01:04:41,467
- [Crow] Just got red contacts.

1491
01:04:41,467 --> 01:04:42,333
What do ya think?

1492
01:04:42,333 --> 01:04:43,967
(dramatic music)
- Nice.

1493
01:04:43,967 --> 01:04:47,200
- [Mike] Wow, lieutenant modest
crotch on the right here.

1494
01:04:47,200 --> 01:04:49,367
- Nothing further
you can do now.

1495
01:04:49,367 --> 01:04:51,933
- [Tom] Well, they've
forgotten your Moo Goo Gai Pan.

1496
01:04:52,900 --> 01:04:54,867
- The creatures got through.

1497
01:04:54,867 --> 01:04:57,033
Smashed the nets.

1498
01:04:57,033 --> 01:04:58,667
- Get me river patrol.

1499
01:04:58,667 --> 01:04:59,600
(dramatic music)

1500
01:04:59,600 --> 01:05:00,833
- [Tom] Uh-oh.

1501
01:05:00,833 --> 01:05:02,100
- [Crow] Hey hey
hey, it's Fat Albert.

1502
01:05:04,400 --> 01:05:05,833
- [Mike] Now is this
the mom or the dad?

1503
01:05:05,833 --> 01:05:07,067
- [Tom] I don't know.

1504
01:05:07,067 --> 01:05:08,300
How do you sex one
of these things?

1505
01:05:08,300 --> 01:05:09,467
- [Crow] Well, you put
the lights down low,

1506
01:05:09,467 --> 01:05:11,300
you put on a little Barry White.

1507
01:05:11,300 --> 01:05:12,600
- [Tom] That's not what I mean.

1508
01:05:12,600 --> 01:05:14,100
That's ridiculous.

1509
01:05:15,100 --> 01:05:16,600
(speaking foreign language)

1510
01:05:16,600 --> 01:05:18,067
- [Mike] Let's get outta here.

1511
01:05:18,067 --> 01:05:20,667
- [Man] You can see the
huge beast still advancing.

1512
01:05:20,667 --> 01:05:22,300
Second line of defense
has been organized,

1513
01:05:22,300 --> 01:05:23,233
greatly reinforced.

1514
01:05:24,233 --> 01:05:28,000
(doors creaking and closing)

1515
01:05:36,900 --> 01:05:38,600
- Oh hey, what's this?

1516
01:05:38,600 --> 01:05:41,467
- Looks like the nanites made
them self a little circus.

1517
01:05:41,467 --> 01:05:42,833
- Oh, they must've been
watching the movie,

1518
01:05:42,833 --> 01:05:44,633
the little dickenses.

1519
01:05:44,633 --> 01:05:46,267
- Well, let's see.

1520
01:05:46,267 --> 01:05:49,600
Hello nanites, what's
going on down there?

1521
01:05:49,600 --> 01:05:52,467
(cheerful music)

1522
01:05:52,467 --> 01:05:55,100
- [Tom] Ladies and gentlemen!

1523
01:05:55,100 --> 01:05:57,267
Children of all ages!

1524
01:05:57,267 --> 01:05:59,333
Come to the
spectacular, gargantuan,

1525
01:05:59,333 --> 01:06:01,667
nanite protos and
darkened combined,

1526
01:06:01,667 --> 01:06:03,733
avionic circus and
carnival of souls.

1527
01:06:03,733 --> 01:06:05,333
- [Crow] With Nate the clown.

1528
01:06:05,333 --> 01:06:08,600
- [Tom] Marvel at the spectacle
of wild subcelluar animals.

1529
01:06:08,600 --> 01:06:10,033
- [Crow] With Nate the Clown.

1530
01:06:10,033 --> 01:06:12,100
- [Tom] Thrill at the daring
of the Nanitino family

1531
01:06:12,100 --> 01:06:15,200
as they fly a full 15
mic-grounds about center ring.

1532
01:06:15,200 --> 01:06:16,667
- [Crow] And Nate the Clown.

1533
01:06:16,667 --> 01:06:18,367
- Whoa, it sounds great.

1534
01:06:18,367 --> 01:06:20,067
How much does it cost?

1535
01:06:20,067 --> 01:06:21,333
- [Tom] I'm not done yet.

1536
01:06:21,333 --> 01:06:24,100
Gasp in horror at the
largely ironic midway

1537
01:06:24,100 --> 01:06:28,200
and squeal with delight at
the antics of Nate the Clown.

1538
01:06:28,200 --> 01:06:31,333
- [Crow] Hey, that's
me. (laughing)

1539
01:06:34,400 --> 01:06:36,700
- Okay, okay, you sold me.

1540
01:06:36,700 --> 01:06:37,567
How much?

1541
01:06:38,733 --> 01:06:41,133
- [Tom] Only 25 cents!

1542
01:06:41,133 --> 01:06:42,600
That's right, a quarter.

1543
01:06:42,600 --> 01:06:46,767
Two bits, five nickels,
one quarter of a greenback.

1544
01:06:46,767 --> 01:06:48,300
- Well, I've got a
handful of quarters.

1545
01:06:48,300 --> 01:06:49,133
You guys in?

1546
01:06:49,133 --> 01:06:50,367
- You bet.

1547
01:06:50,367 --> 01:06:51,333
- I want to see
the ironic midway.

1548
01:06:51,333 --> 01:06:52,533
- Here you go, pallie.

1549
01:06:53,467 --> 01:06:54,933
(Tom shouts)

1550
01:06:54,933 --> 01:06:56,833
- [Tom] Oh horrors, the curse
of the circus is upon us.

1551
01:06:56,833 --> 01:07:01,067
Giant stones fall from the
sky, and our clown is dead.

1552
01:07:02,500 --> 01:07:03,433
- [Crow] Ah, the microbes
are on the loose.

1553
01:07:03,433 --> 01:07:04,667
The ameobas are on the rampage.

1554
01:07:04,667 --> 01:07:05,900
Run!

1555
01:07:05,900 --> 01:07:09,267
(Tom shouting)

1556
01:07:09,267 --> 01:07:10,300
- Ow, sorry.

1557
01:07:10,300 --> 01:07:11,600
I was just tossing one on.

1558
01:07:12,867 --> 01:07:13,700
- [Tom] Here comes another.

1559
01:07:13,700 --> 01:07:14,900
The circus is destroyed!

1560
01:07:16,933 --> 01:07:18,800
- Maybe we should go
to the zoo instead.

1561
01:07:18,800 --> 01:07:20,067
- That's a good idea.

1562
01:07:20,067 --> 01:07:21,067
We'll be right back.

1563
01:07:21,067 --> 01:07:22,600
- Clown killer.

1564
01:07:22,600 --> 01:07:23,433
- Maybe.

1565
01:07:25,067 --> 01:07:27,633
(upbeat music)

1566
01:07:31,633 --> 01:07:34,733
- [Crow] Geez, did ya
see that clown blood?

1567
01:07:34,733 --> 01:07:36,767
- [Mike] Whoopsy.

1568
01:07:36,767 --> 01:07:38,833
- [Tom] I don't think the
sanctions are working.

1569
01:07:40,467 --> 01:07:42,800
- Get your lines ready, sir.

1570
01:07:44,267 --> 01:07:46,367
- Open the valves.

1571
01:07:46,367 --> 01:07:49,100
- [Crow] Ah, they're gonna get
it drunk on woodpecker cider.

1572
01:07:52,333 --> 01:07:55,400
♪ Downtown ♪

1573
01:07:55,400 --> 01:07:56,733
- [Man] Come on, hop to it.

1574
01:07:58,067 --> 01:08:00,433
- [Tom] McHale's Navy
valiantly battles the creature.

1575
01:08:02,567 --> 01:08:05,233
(Gorgo roaring)

1576
01:08:09,333 --> 01:08:12,667
- [Crow] Come on,
it's the 5:15 monster.

1577
01:08:14,833 --> 01:08:15,800
- Be ready.

1578
01:08:15,800 --> 01:08:17,733
Let's get behind those sandbags.

1579
01:08:17,733 --> 01:08:20,600
- [Mike] This feels
good, squishing the
mud between my toes.

1580
01:08:20,600 --> 01:08:21,433
- Fire!

1581
01:08:21,433 --> 01:08:22,267
(gunshot bangs)

1582
01:08:22,267 --> 01:08:23,200
- [Tom] Got him.

1583
01:08:23,200 --> 01:08:24,000
He's dead, it's over, let's go.

1584
01:08:24,000 --> 01:08:24,967
- [Mike] Good, let's go.

1585
01:08:26,000 --> 01:08:27,233
- [Crow] This is a fine welcome.

1586
01:08:27,233 --> 01:08:29,500
They treated Jerry Lee
Lewis better than this.

1587
01:08:31,333 --> 01:08:33,433
- [Mike] Bollocks, Gorgo.

1588
01:08:38,367 --> 01:08:40,467
- [Crow] Now that just
seemed completely avoidable.

1589
01:08:47,600 --> 01:08:48,933
- Get me army headquarters.

1590
01:08:50,433 --> 01:08:53,000
- Atomic weapons are out
of the question. (mumbling)

1591
01:08:53,000 --> 01:08:54,300
- [Tom] Wacko!

1592
01:08:54,300 --> 01:08:55,800
- Tanks? Of course.

1593
01:08:55,800 --> 01:08:57,300
- [Man] Move out, move out!

1594
01:08:57,300 --> 01:08:58,467
Up you go.

1595
01:08:58,467 --> 01:08:59,700
- [Mike] Come on, let's
do something easy,

1596
01:08:59,700 --> 01:09:01,600
like put down some
Arabs or something.

1597
01:09:05,333 --> 01:09:06,267
- [Tom] These things are great.

1598
01:09:06,267 --> 01:09:07,933
They're built like Volvos.

1599
01:09:07,933 --> 01:09:09,033
- Attention.

1600
01:09:09,033 --> 01:09:10,900
This is a special bulletin.

1601
01:09:10,900 --> 01:09:12,967
Civil defense
authorities have declared

1602
01:09:12,967 --> 01:09:15,433
a state of emergency
for all London areas

1603
01:09:15,433 --> 01:09:17,633
within three miles
of the River Thames.

1604
01:09:17,633 --> 01:09:18,800
- [Crow] Ah, he'll
probably be sated on people

1605
01:09:18,800 --> 01:09:20,133
from the suburbs by then.

1606
01:09:28,000 --> 01:09:29,533
- [Tom] There's a
sale on bumber shoots.

1607
01:09:29,533 --> 01:09:32,833
(man mumbling)

1608
01:09:32,833 --> 01:09:33,833
- [Crow] See, not so bad.

1609
01:09:33,833 --> 01:09:34,833
Keep your chin up.

1610
01:09:34,833 --> 01:09:35,667
Stiff upper lip.

1611
01:09:38,033 --> 01:09:39,600
- [Man] The streets
must be kept clear

1612
01:09:39,600 --> 01:09:42,700
for military and
defense personnel.

1613
01:09:42,700 --> 01:09:44,233
- [Mike] And Good Humor trucks.

1614
01:09:47,000 --> 01:09:50,567
- [Tom] Ah, the queen mum is
brought out to fight the beast.

1615
01:09:50,567 --> 01:09:51,767
Hello.

1616
01:09:51,767 --> 01:09:52,600
Hello.

1617
01:09:58,533 --> 01:10:01,867
(shouting indistinctly)

1618
01:10:03,433 --> 01:10:04,500
- [Tom] Yes, sir.

1619
01:10:05,900 --> 01:10:07,367
- [Crow] Being in the
British army is a cinch.

1620
01:10:07,367 --> 01:10:10,333
You just hold a rifle,
run around, and look busy.

1621
01:10:10,333 --> 01:10:11,267
- Come on.

1622
01:10:11,267 --> 01:10:12,733
- [Man] Get off the streets.

1623
01:10:12,733 --> 01:10:13,933
- [Crow] Okay.

1624
01:10:13,933 --> 01:10:16,067
- [Mike] No, no he's
talking to guys.

1625
01:10:16,067 --> 01:10:18,567
- [Man] You will be notified
when the emergency is over.

1626
01:10:18,567 --> 01:10:21,300
Get off the streets
and go to your homes.

1627
01:10:21,300 --> 01:10:25,967
- [Mike] Now tanks are brought
in to roll over the doll.

1628
01:10:28,600 --> 01:10:29,667
- [Crow] Yeah, this is
the same thing they do

1629
01:10:29,667 --> 01:10:31,133
when Oliver Reid's in town.

1630
01:10:35,933 --> 01:10:38,133
- [Tom] Can't believe
we all missed the bus.

1631
01:10:39,733 --> 01:10:40,967
- [Man] Up the ramp there.

1632
01:10:40,967 --> 01:10:41,800
Move in, move in.

1633
01:10:41,800 --> 01:10:43,800
Take your positions.

1634
01:10:43,800 --> 01:10:45,267
Parting positions!

1635
01:10:50,067 --> 01:10:51,333
- It's advancing up river
directly towards us.

1636
01:10:51,333 --> 01:10:52,333
- [Crow] Stay here
and get killed?

1637
01:10:52,333 --> 01:10:53,267
Yes sir.

1638
01:10:53,267 --> 01:10:55,100
- Very good, sir.

1639
01:10:55,100 --> 01:10:56,567
- [Tom] A woman.

1640
01:10:56,567 --> 01:10:57,967
- Although the army
indicates full confidence,

1641
01:10:57,967 --> 01:11:00,500
they report growing
casualties among their forces.

1642
01:11:00,500 --> 01:11:03,000
We are told that Downing Street
is in constant communication

1643
01:11:03,000 --> 01:11:05,800
with other world capitals, but
grave concern is expressed.

1644
01:11:05,800 --> 01:11:08,067
- [Mike] Though I detected
a snicker in their voice.

1645
01:11:08,067 --> 01:11:09,200
(whistle blows)

1646
01:11:09,200 --> 01:11:10,500
- Call headquarters.

1647
01:11:10,500 --> 01:11:11,900
Shoot the flares!

1648
01:11:11,900 --> 01:11:13,300
(gunshot bangs)

1649
01:11:13,300 --> 01:11:14,800
Open side fire!

1650
01:11:14,800 --> 01:11:15,633
Standby!

1651
01:11:16,667 --> 01:11:18,667
Commence fire!

1652
01:11:18,667 --> 01:11:19,500
- [Crow] Hey, wait,
I don't have my gun!

1653
01:11:19,500 --> 01:11:20,633
Oh, I see.

1654
01:11:20,633 --> 01:11:23,567
(gunshots banging)

1655
01:11:28,767 --> 01:11:31,500
- [Mike] You're the ones who
sold me my anti-missile armor.

1656
01:11:36,033 --> 01:11:38,833
- [Crow] Ah this is really
cleaning my pores, thank you.

1657
01:11:40,833 --> 01:11:42,267
- [Tom] Now Mike,
you're probably gripped

1658
01:11:42,267 --> 01:11:43,767
with the urge to say London
Bridge is falling down

1659
01:11:43,767 --> 01:11:45,200
at this point, but don't, don't!

1660
01:11:45,200 --> 01:11:46,867
This is actually
the Tower Bridge.

1661
01:11:48,467 --> 01:11:50,167
♪ London Bridge
is falling down ♪

1662
01:11:50,167 --> 01:11:51,533
- [Tom] Mike, what
did I just tell you?

1663
01:11:51,533 --> 01:11:52,367
- [Mike] What?

1664
01:11:52,367 --> 01:11:53,267
Oh, right, sorry.

1665
01:11:59,700 --> 01:12:02,367
(Gorgo roaring)

1666
01:12:02,367 --> 01:12:04,867
(Crow shouts)

1667
01:12:06,133 --> 01:12:07,300
- [Mike] Why, that's
quite bracing,

1668
01:12:07,300 --> 01:12:08,833
getting knocked off the bridge.

1669
01:12:12,567 --> 01:12:13,800
- [Tom] Ooh, red ants!

1670
01:12:13,800 --> 01:12:15,500
- The bridge is gone!

1671
01:12:15,500 --> 01:12:18,533
One of London's oldest
landmarks, smashed
like matchwood.

1672
01:12:18,533 --> 01:12:21,333
Nothing can stop this
beast so far, nothing.

1673
01:12:21,333 --> 01:12:23,733
Not even point blank canon fire.

1674
01:12:23,733 --> 01:12:24,567
- [Tom] Not women.

1675
01:12:24,567 --> 01:12:26,100
- The entire bridge?

1676
01:12:26,100 --> 01:12:27,933
Then the power of this
thing is fantastic.

1677
01:12:27,933 --> 01:12:30,167
- [Tom] And I'm on
half power as it is!

1678
01:12:34,267 --> 01:12:35,500
(Gorgo roaring)

1679
01:12:35,500 --> 01:12:37,400
- [Crow] Hi, I'm Gorgo,
but I'm not Mormo

1680
01:12:37,400 --> 01:12:38,767
of the thousand-faced moon.

1681
01:12:44,300 --> 01:12:47,100
- [Tom] Maybe Mary Poppins
flies in and kicks his ass.

1682
01:12:47,100 --> 01:12:48,567
- [Mike] That I'd pay to see.

1683
01:12:55,133 --> 01:12:57,500
No, he has the power
to superimpose rocks.

1684
01:13:02,600 --> 01:13:03,633
- Check the missile units.

1685
01:13:03,633 --> 01:13:04,467
- Yes sir.

1686
01:13:11,200 --> 01:13:13,800
- [Crow] Cliff Richards is
opening up the palladium.

1687
01:13:15,333 --> 01:13:16,600
- [Tom] Juggling
pins I brought in,

1688
01:13:16,600 --> 01:13:17,800
and the monster is
challenged to keep

1689
01:13:17,800 --> 01:13:19,233
five up in the air at once.

1690
01:13:22,033 --> 01:13:24,100
- Meanwhile, the Russians
slip in the back door,

1691
01:13:24,100 --> 01:13:26,733
take over London, and
rename it Khrushchevgrad.

1692
01:13:28,767 --> 01:13:31,333
- [Crow] They could fling
really hot curry in his eyes.

1693
01:13:31,333 --> 01:13:33,700
- [Tom] Yeah, or spray
him with malt vinegar.

1694
01:13:33,700 --> 01:13:34,533
- Back, back, come back!

1695
01:13:35,600 --> 01:13:37,667
- Yes sir, I will.

1696
01:13:37,667 --> 01:13:38,600
40 degrees!

1697
01:13:41,867 --> 01:13:43,400
- [Crow] Hey, it's Large Bob.

1698
01:13:43,400 --> 01:13:44,500
- [Mike] It's Big Ben.

1699
01:13:44,500 --> 01:13:45,667
- [Crow] Oh sure, Mike.

1700
01:13:50,867 --> 01:13:53,533
(Gorgo roaring)

1701
01:13:55,233 --> 01:13:56,467
- [Tom] Think it's noticeable?

1702
01:13:56,467 --> 01:13:57,533
- [Mike] Nah.

1703
01:13:58,533 --> 01:13:59,867
- [Tom] Oh boy.

1704
01:13:59,867 --> 01:14:01,233
- [Crow] Big Ben.

1705
01:14:02,600 --> 01:14:04,600
- [Mike] Ah, it was running
a little fast anyway.

1706
01:14:09,767 --> 01:14:12,700
(dramatic music)

1707
01:14:12,700 --> 01:14:15,233
- [Crow] Got a few people
caught in my teeth.

1708
01:14:15,233 --> 01:14:18,400
(Gorgo screeching)

1709
01:14:18,400 --> 01:14:19,233
- Come on.

1710
01:14:20,400 --> 01:14:22,433
- [Tom] I want access
to the Law Library.

1711
01:14:23,833 --> 01:14:26,000
- Burned out our connection.

1712
01:14:26,000 --> 01:14:27,267
- [Mike] Here, let's
check is air pressure.

1713
01:14:27,267 --> 01:14:29,200
Maybe that's the problem.

1714
01:14:31,100 --> 01:14:32,433
- Where's Shawn?

1715
01:14:34,433 --> 01:14:35,567
- [Crow] Ah, General Shawn.

1716
01:14:35,567 --> 01:14:36,467
Glad we found you.

1717
01:14:40,733 --> 01:14:42,167
- Shawn!

1718
01:14:42,167 --> 01:14:43,667
Come on.

1719
01:14:43,667 --> 01:14:45,233
- [Tom] Come on, we have to
rescue our child that we hate.

1720
01:14:49,700 --> 01:14:53,167
- All homes in this area
must be evacuated at once.

1721
01:14:53,167 --> 01:14:54,400
- [Mike] The Darlings.

1722
01:14:54,400 --> 01:14:56,067
- You have no time to
remove your belongings.

1723
01:14:56,067 --> 01:14:57,600
Get out of the area.

1724
01:14:57,600 --> 01:14:59,300
Proceed in a westerly direction.

1725
01:15:00,767 --> 01:15:03,400
- [Crow] Well, the good
news is he may crush Sting.

1726
01:15:03,400 --> 01:15:04,300
- [Tom] You think so?

1727
01:15:04,300 --> 01:15:05,233
- [Crow] Could be.

1728
01:15:05,233 --> 01:15:06,500
- [Mike] Good news for everyone.

1729
01:15:08,067 --> 01:15:09,967
- [Tom] A lot of people
will end up in hospital

1730
01:15:09,967 --> 01:15:13,133
because of this, perhaps
even people from university.

1731
01:15:16,200 --> 01:15:19,033
(Mike vocalizing)

1732
01:15:24,900 --> 01:15:27,700
- [Crow] Oh, not her majesty's
certified paper clip works,

1733
01:15:27,700 --> 01:15:31,167
purveyors of paper clip to her
majesty's court since 1743.

1734
01:15:31,167 --> 01:15:34,567
(car horn honking)

1735
01:15:34,567 --> 01:15:36,933
- [Tom] It's the London
landmark Banco Espanol.

1736
01:15:41,267 --> 01:15:43,467
- [Mike] Hang on, I
gotta change some pesos.

1737
01:15:47,867 --> 01:15:49,800
- [Crow] It's a Lulu
autograph session.

1738
01:15:59,200 --> 01:16:00,833
- Run that way, Sam.

1739
01:16:00,833 --> 01:16:01,933
We'll meet back at Battersea!

1740
01:16:01,933 --> 01:16:03,033
- [Mike] Great, about four?

1741
01:16:03,033 --> 01:16:04,100
I'll bring some beer.

1742
01:16:09,200 --> 01:16:11,200
(people screaming)

1743
01:16:11,200 --> 01:16:12,933
- [Tom] What, what,
am I offending?

1744
01:16:14,900 --> 01:16:17,033
- [Mike] Maybe he just
hates Georgian architecture.

1745
01:16:17,033 --> 01:16:18,200
- [Tom] Maybe.

1746
01:16:19,900 --> 01:16:22,200
- [Crow] Oof, I hope
that's not a mating call.

1747
01:16:26,900 --> 01:16:29,200
- [Tom] I'm glad you're
killing Mother, Gorgo.

1748
